WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.750
- Hi, good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.200
My name is Priya Natarajan.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.930
I'm an astrophysicist

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.910
and serve as the current
director of the Franke Program

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:15.320
in Science and the Humanities at Yale.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.480
I am delighted to invite you all

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.583
to a monthly inference colloquium series.

00:00:20.583 --> 00:00:24.470
Though first I would like to
recognize our benefactors,

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.310
Mr. And Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.710
who are on the call
today and have joined us.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.601
And I also wanna remind all of you

00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:36.190
that we are recording this event

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.980
and the recording is made
available within a week or so.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.601
And since we are recording,

00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:46.590
we are required to have all
participants mute their videos.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:50.010
And if you wish to
participate in the Q and A,

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.670
which we encourage, we would
like you to submit questions

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:54.900
through the chat feature

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.350
and feel free to submit
your questions throughout.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.730
Although we will handle the questions

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:05.730
and the speaker will address
them at the end of their talk.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:11.660
So I am absolutely delighted
today to introduce our speaker,

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.250
Professor James Owen Weatherall,

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:20.020
who is a Professor of Logic
and Philosophy of Science

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.490
at the University of California Irvine.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:23.780
But he's also a member

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.910
of the Institute for
Mathematical Behavioral Sciences

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.570
and Center for Cosmology.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:34.540
And as you all know, part of the goal

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.100
for this series is to
really try to understand,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.180
explore and understand issues related

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.440
to epistemic models,
correlation, causation

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.440
and we could not have a better speaker

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:53.550
than today's to provide deep insights

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.100
into what models really do.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:01.100
And over his career, he has
looked at a range of questions.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.780
Very, very interesting,
very topical questions.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.930
His most recent book is
titled, The Misinformation Age,

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.360
How False Beliefs Spread,

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.510
and which is coauthored
with Cailin O'Connor

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:16.550
in which he sort of
explores the social dynamics

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:18.530
and this parent, if you will,

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.120
of belief in evidence rich environments.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.590
And he has several previous books as well.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.090
One on the void, which is the first one

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.750
of his that I actually read

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.950
and thoroughly enjoyed
that was published in 2016

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.830
by Yale University Press.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.870
His current research
interests as I mentioned,

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:41.898
span a range of very, very
relevant issues that have to do

00:02:41.898 --> 00:02:46.650
with mathematical and conceptual
foundations of physics,

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.510
philosophy of cosmology

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.763
and social epidemiology.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.300
Today, he will be talking to us

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.720
about polarization of beliefs.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:59.380
In particular on issues where at the gist,

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:02.420
life facts in contrast to opinions.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.770
So one striking feature, as
you have all probably noted

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.340
of the current political
environment in the United States

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.080
and elsewhere is that
they're a large groups

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.390
of people with extremely divergent beliefs

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:18.540
about what are in fact
testable matters of fact.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.740
So these are matters of fact,

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.310
whether it is a rich and
widely accessible body

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:27.680
of scientific evidence that
will clearly support one side

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:28.990
over the other.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:32.490
And despite that, there
is a raging dispute.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.450
And so, you know, we have
all seen this manifested

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:39.300
in the political environment,
with the elections

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.070
and the aftermath of the elections,

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:44.170
as well as the unfolding global pandemic

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:45.870
and all the public health

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:49.540
and medical issues that
have arisen around that.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.610
And we have seen in
many ways, the you know,

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.080
in the context of the
provisional nature of science,

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.550
we've sort of seen this interplay
of the honing and refining

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:04.960
of facts and data and
evidence and belief formation.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.260
We've all sort of had a front row seat

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.480
to how some of these processes occur,

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.210
especially in this past year.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:12.550
In his talk today,

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:15.160
James will draw on recent modeling work

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.010
to provide three distinct
possible explanations

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.160
for this polarization in belief.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.150
And each of these explanations invokes

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:28.310
very different mechanisms and
recommends different remedies.

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:31.470
He will conclude with a
discussion of what it means

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.630
from the perspective of epistemology

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.510
of a model based science

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:37.670
that such different models can produce

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.610
such similar outcomes.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.530
And the moral of his talk
today will be to caution us

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:47.140
against very overly
simplistic explanations

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.600
and understand that these are
issues of great complexity.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.860
And therefore, there are
no silver bullet solutions

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.000
to our current quandary, sort of living

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.220
in an environment which is
rampant with misinformation,

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:05.180
disinformation, and rampant
denial of scientific facts.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.520
And I just wanna mention
that as for custom,

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.470
tomorrow we will host a conversation

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.950
between Professor Weatherall
and Professor Kevin Dorst

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:16.480
of the Department of Philosophy

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:19.960
from the University of
Pittsburgh and at the same time,

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.540
so may 26 tomorrow at 3:00 PM.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:26.253
And so we are really, really
looking forward, James,

00:05:26.253 --> 00:05:28.490
to your talk today, please take it away.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:29.700
And thank you so much

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:32.693
to both of you for
accepting this invitation.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:35.770
- Thank you so much Priya

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:39.870
and thank you for your work
in organizing the series.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:43.270
I've enjoyed the other talks
and glad to get to participate.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:47.343
So I'm gonna share a screen here.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:54.180
Great, so just gonna jump right in.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.220
I'm gonna talk today about polarization

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.820
and so let me just be clear right up front

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:02.620
what I mean by polarization

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.720
because there are a few different kinds

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:09.720
of phenomenon that arise in,
you know, in this context

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.210
that sometimes get this name.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.110
So what I have in mind

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:19.610
when I talk about polarization
today is a situation

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:24.610
in which you have a community
of epistemic agents.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:28.430
So by that, I mean a community

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:33.430
of people who are trying to
learn about how the world is.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.380
And so polarization arises

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:43.150
in such a community
when the various members

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:47.710
of the community come to hold beliefs

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.180
that diverge from one another.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.980
So I'm talking about
situations where agents come

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:56.637
to hold divergent beliefs
about matters of fact.

00:06:56.637 --> 00:06:59.260
And I wanna be very
clear when I say belief,

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.470
I'm not talking about religious belief,

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:06.450
I'm not talking about assertions
about values, what matters,

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.350
whether something is important or not.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:14.200
What I'm talking about are
credences about matters of fact.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:19.220
So the amount of confidence that you have

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:23.143
that some assertion about
the world is true or false.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.100
Now, polarization apparently obtains

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.941
in real world situations.

00:07:29.941 --> 00:07:34.600
There are many examples just we've seen

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:35.900
in the last several years.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:39.080
For instance, you pose the question,

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.910
was there widespread voter fraud.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:43.930
Widespread is too fuzzy a word.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.270
Was there voter fraud

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.590
at a scale sufficient
to influence the outcome

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:51.302
of the 2020 US election?

00:07:51.302 --> 00:07:53.870
Considerable amounts of
polling data suggests

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:56.710
that there really is a divergent.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:01.040
There really are divergent
beliefs in the US population

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:04.420
about that question with
a great deal of confidence

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:07.790
on both sides about what the facts are.

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:10.473
So this is the kind of
situation that I have in mind.

00:08:12.249 --> 00:08:15.840
And I'm focusing here as Priya mentioned

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:19.068
in the introduction, on
cases where it's not just

00:08:19.068 --> 00:08:20.792
that there are these
disagreements, but in fact,

00:08:20.792 --> 00:08:22.190
there appears to be information available

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:27.190
to the community that bears
on the situation in the sense

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:31.740
that there should be evidence,
there's evidence available.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:35.010
Prima facie, it appears
that that evidence ought

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.700
to be sufficient to settle the
issue one way or the other.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.210
And yet, nonetheless,

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:43.713
it doesn't seem as if consensus emerges.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:49.460
Okay, and so what I'm
gonna do today is talk

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:51.640
about three different models

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:54.490
in which polarization
is a possible outcome.

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:55.740
Is there are agent-based models

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:59.110
in which I will describe

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:02.470
how sort of very simplified setting,

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:06.270
idealized agents gather information

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:08.570
about the world and share that information

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:10.720
and then update their beliefs in light

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.720
of that information and show that

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:19.823
in each of these models,
polarization is a possible outcome.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.410
Now, each of these
models can be understood

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:28.280
to suggest a prima facie
plausible explanation

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:29.700
of real-world polarization.

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:30.533
These are the kinds

00:09:30.533 --> 00:09:34.900
of explanations that you
sometimes see theorized

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:38.713
as the explanation for actual
real-world polarization,

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:43.020
but as I'll discuss, each
of the models differs

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.052
in the kind of explanation that they offer

00:09:46.052 --> 00:09:50.790
and in the details of the conditions

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:53.823
under which you should expect
polarization to appear.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:58.380
And then, so you know, most

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:01.477
of the talk is gonna be
discussing these various models

00:10:01.477 --> 00:10:05.240
and what kinds of explanations
of polarization they suggest.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:06.430
But I'm gonna conclude

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:11.430
by thinking about some issues
and philosophy of modeling,

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.760
about what we can infer
from this situation.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:18.330
What we can infer from the
fact that a single phenomenon,

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:19.360
a single social phenomenon,

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:22.030
in this case could be plausibly modeled

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:24.233
in such different ways.

00:10:25.746 --> 00:10:27.690
I now acknowledge my a collaborator.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:30.250
So I'm gonna be discussing
three different papers.

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:32.480
Two of which have appeared in print.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:34.924
One of which is unpublished.

00:10:34.924 --> 00:10:39.924
Cailin O'Connor is a co-author
on all of these papers.

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:42.570
Also co-author of the
book that Priya mentioned,

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:43.913
Misinformation Age,

00:10:44.804 --> 00:10:46.913
where some of these results are discussed.

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:54.130
Okay, so we discussed the
modeling methodology briefly.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:58.350
So, as I said, I'm gonna
discuss here agent-based models.

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:03.020
I wanna emphasize these are
highly idealized models.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:07.313
These are not meant to
provide a fully accurate,

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:13.140
complete representation

00:11:14.089 --> 00:11:16.653
of any kind of human activity.

00:11:17.549 --> 00:11:22.549
They're supposed to abstract
some particular collection

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.920
of salient features that
one might ask whether,

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:29.843
one might ask

00:11:29.843 --> 00:11:32.360
whether those features are sufficient

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:34.860
under some circumstances to bring

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:38.483
about some sort of qualitative phenomenon.

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:42.430
And so we're reasoning

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:45.440
with highly abstract and
highly idealized models.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:47.700
I'm gonna describe how the models work,

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:49.940
so hopefully you'll get
a sense of how this works

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:52.130
but I just wanna flag
upfront that we aren't trying

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:57.130
to give various
similitudiness representations

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:02.190
of the actual, you
know, social world here.

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:05.100
We're doing something a little
bit different from that.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.710
Two of the models that
I'm gonna discuss are

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:10.310
within the same modeling framework

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:13.060
known as the network
epistemology framework

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:16.683
and then the third considers
individual Bayesian learning.

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:20.440
Now each project establishes conditions

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:22.800
under which polarization may emerge.

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:25.330
Now, I absolutely do not wanna suggest

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:28.960
that these are the only
models in which this happens

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:30.430
or the most important models

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:34.223
in which this happens
or anything like that.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.423
They're representative in a sense,

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:40.941
but I'm describing some of the
models that I've worked on.

00:12:40.941 --> 00:12:44.030
There are many, many other
models, some very similar,

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:44.863
some very different

00:12:44.863 --> 00:12:47.100
from the ones I'm gonna discuss that lead

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:53.113
to very similar sorts of
arguments and observations.

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:57.530
Okay, good.

00:12:57.530 --> 00:13:01.210
So I'm gonna begin by
giving a sort of an overview

00:13:02.113 --> 00:13:06.260
of the framework in which
the first two models lives,

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:08.330
the network, the epistemology framework.

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:12.080
I'm then going to introduce two versions,

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:15.713
two network epistemology
models that I'm gonna discuss.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:20.320
One based on what I'm
gonna call trust dynamics,

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.520
one based on what I'm
gonna call conformity.

00:13:23.399 --> 00:13:25.959
And then I'm gonna
introduce the third model,

00:13:25.959 --> 00:13:28.510
works in a somewhat different framework

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:33.410
and discuss how that can be
used to understand some aspects

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.750
of journalistic practices.

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:37.960
And then I'm gonna
conclude with a discussion

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:41.623
of what we can learn from
this modeling situation.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:44.773
So to begin, network of epistemology.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:49.470
So to build the models

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:52.920
on the first two of
these parts of the talk,

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:56.300
we work in a framework that
was originally developed

00:13:56.300 --> 00:14:00.803
by two economists, Venkatesh
Bala and Sanjeev Goyal.

00:14:02.577 --> 00:14:05.300
They were working with an audience

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:08.830
of economists in mind,
but their work was adapted

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:13.830
to the philosophy of science
context about 15 years ago now

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:19.140
by Kevin Zollman who is the
one on the far left there.

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:24.660
So since then, there's been a community

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:28.000
of people working in philosophy
of science using this sort

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.640
of framework to try to
understand various aspects

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.300
of the social spread of evidence

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:36.740
and belief within epistemic communities,

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:40.853
generally and specifically
within scientific communities.

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:47.160
And so the way these models work is

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:50.690
that you have a decision problem.

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:53.140
So basically you're trying to choose

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:56.690
between two possible actions.

00:14:56.690 --> 00:15:00.790
We're gonna call one action
A and the other one action B.

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:03.670
Now the setup here is that
action A and action B,

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:07.463
when they succeed,
provide the same payoff,

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:11.160
but they succeed at different rates.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:12.530
And so you're uncertain

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:14.910
about which one has a
higher expected value

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:15.890
because you're uncertain

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:19.143
about which one's exceeds
at the higher rate.

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:22.590
So we're gonna assume here that

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.920
in fact one of these actions
has been well-studied

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:28.281
and all of the agents are confident

00:15:28.281 --> 00:15:33.281
that that action is,
succeeds at a rate of 0.5.

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:35.340
So half the time it succeeds,

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:37.320
it gives payoff one,
half the time it fails

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:38.470
and it gives no payoff.

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:40.920
But they're uncertain about the rate

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:45.530
at which the other
action, action B succeeds.

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:46.930
And they're trying to learn

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:49.890
about whether actually B
has a higher expected value

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:54.890
or a lower expected value by testing.

00:15:55.379 --> 00:16:00.379
They're performing, they
perform the various actions.

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:03.440
They learn based on what happens

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:04.940
when they do perform the actions.

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:07.690
They update their beliefs
about which action is better.

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:10.210
And so there's a whole
community trying to converge

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:15.210
to all act in the way that
yields the higher expected value.

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:18.738
Now, this is a very
simple problem of course.

00:16:18.738 --> 00:16:19.760
There's certain kinds

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:24.620
of scientific situations that
have this qualitative feature.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:26.910
So for instance, you might think

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:31.910
about a situation where a
new medication is introduced.

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:35.198
And if that medication succeeds,

00:16:35.198 --> 00:16:38.780
it has the outcome that
a patient survives.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.750
And if it fails, the
patient does not survive.

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:44.320
You're gonna have to, you know,
you're trying to compare now

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:47.330
is this new medication
more or less successful

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:48.810
than some existing medication

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.483
whose success rate is well-known.

00:16:52.831 --> 00:16:55.316
One thing that's nice
about this particular kind

00:16:55.316 --> 00:16:57.850
of example, is they're pretty high stakes.

00:16:57.850 --> 00:16:59.770
So you wanna perform whatever action

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:03.740
you think is the better one,
even though you're of course,

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:07.160
very, very interested
in learning from others,

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:10.320
whether or not perhaps
the other action turns out

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:11.543
to be better after all.

00:17:12.939 --> 00:17:16.760
Okay, and so this is the problem

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:18.723
that our agents are trying to solve.

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:23.260
We represent the agents
as having credences.

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:25.950
So these credences can be represented

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:30.380
as a single number between zero and one.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:33.950
We can think of that as the
probability that the agent,

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:38.950
each agent assigns to the
proposition that B is better.

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:42.711
Now, I should say, in all of these models,

00:17:42.711 --> 00:17:45.750
we're just adopting a convention
that in fact B is better.

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:50.137
This is important because there
really is a better outcome.

00:17:50.137 --> 00:17:52.420
You know, there's a fact of the matter.

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:54.770
There's some truth here
that we can know about

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:57.110
outside the model and
which we can observe now

00:17:57.110 --> 00:18:00.750
how the agents learn or
how successful they are

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:05.750
in learning about whether
or not B is in fact better.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:10.900
Okay, and so the agents
are arranged on a network.

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:13.810
So the idea is that each agent

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:17.790
in each round of the
simulation is understood

00:18:17.790 --> 00:18:18.780
to perform an action.

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:21.340
They perform the action
that they think is better

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:23.723
some number of times.

00:18:26.303 --> 00:18:29.080
So if they're, the number

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:31.260
that represents their
credence is greater than 0.5,

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:32.250
they perform action B.

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:35.240
If it's less than 0.5,
they perform action A

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:36.463
some number of times.

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:39.640
After doing that,

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:43.100
you can think of this as a kind
of performing an experiment.

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:46.760
You get some sample and could be one,

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:47.850
but it could be greater than one.

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:50.070
So you could perform the
action several times,

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:51.810
think of that as the
results of an experiment.

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:55.155
And now you take that data

00:18:55.155 --> 00:18:58.980
and each agent updates their belief

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:03.263
in light of that data using Bayes rule.

00:19:05.138 --> 00:19:08.580
Okay, now in addition to
updating, based on their own data,

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:10.920
this is where the network
structure comes in.

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:13.340
This is why this is the
network of epistemology.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:18.010
In addition to updating
their beliefs based

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:19.260
on their own data,

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:22.930
all of the agents also
update their beliefs based

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:26.423
on the data generated by
their immediate neighbors.

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:30.170
And so what happens here is that you have

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:32.960
at least to begin with, different agents

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:36.540
in the network performing
the two different actions,

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:38.598
but then in general,

00:19:38.598 --> 00:19:42.310
all or many of the agents
receiving information

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:44.430
not only about what they
think is the better action,

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:47.280
but also what their neighbors
think is the better action.

00:19:48.195 --> 00:19:51.690
And this way they can update
on both kinds of actions.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:56.690
And eventually they converge
to some sort of outcome.

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:03.130
Now this basic model as I've
just described it is one

00:20:03.130 --> 00:20:05.260
in which generally authors have found

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:09.770
that communities converge
towards consensus.

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:14.650
That is either all of the agents converge

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:17.695
to having very high
confidence that action B is

00:20:17.695 --> 00:20:18.528
in fact better.

00:20:18.528 --> 00:20:20.570
An outcome that we'll call true consensus.

00:20:20.570 --> 00:20:24.713
So the consensus about the truth.

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:27.550
And the other possibility is

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:30.240
that the agents all erroneously settle

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:31.903
on the worst action, A.

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:35.615
But because of the
structure of this model,

00:20:35.615 --> 00:20:37.730
you're gonna get one or the other.

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:39.780
So basically what's going
to happen is eventually all

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:41.590
of the agents are gonna
be performing action B,

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:42.550
which isn't backed better.

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:45.680
And overall the data is going
to tend to keep their beliefs

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:47.277
that B is better, high.

00:20:47.277 --> 00:20:49.860
Or what's going to happen is that all

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:51.570
of them are gonna come to
believe that A is better,

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:53.477
no one will perform action B anymore.

00:20:53.477 --> 00:20:55.650
And so you end up with a stable outcome

00:20:55.650 --> 00:20:56.750
where no new information

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:59.730
about action B is gathered
because everyone has decided

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:03.053
that the worst action is better.

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:09.020
Okay, so that's the basic model
that we're gonna be working

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:12.023
with the first two parts of the talk here.

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:17.230
Now I'm going to discuss the
first of two modifications

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:20.740
to this model that
introduced some component

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:24.907
that might be understood
to explain polarization.

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:29.300
Okay, so this is based
on a paper that Cailin

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:31.310
and I wrote that was published

00:21:31.310 --> 00:21:33.210
in the European Journal
for Philosophy of Science

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:36.273
a few years ago called
Scientific Polarization.

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:41.710
So in this model, what we do is we begin

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:43.600
with the same network
of epistemology model

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:45.520
that I discussed,

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:50.520
but we modify how agents update

00:21:50.950 --> 00:21:54.330
in light of evidence
produced by other agents.

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:56.650
So agents still update in
light of their own evidence

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:59.960
in the same way, but they update

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:04.960
in light of other agents'
evidence in a different way.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:09.545
The idea here that the
motivation for this is

00:22:09.545 --> 00:22:14.545
that we suppose that agents
take some evidence to be

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:18.913
of higher quality than others.

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:25.140
And the higher quality evidence
they're going to take to,

00:22:25.705 --> 00:22:26.538
they're gonna be very confident in.

00:22:26.538 --> 00:22:29.763
Lower quality evidence, they're
going to be less confident.

00:22:30.950 --> 00:22:32.870
So how do they determine what kind

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:35.900
of evidence to be confident in?

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:37.240
Well, of course there are various ways

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:39.540
in which you can imagine
are very complicated,

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:41.330
high rationality sorts of strategies

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:42.430
for figuring this out.

00:22:43.305 --> 00:22:46.020
Here we suppose just a very simple one

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:50.870
which is that the agents
take the data generated

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:55.870
by people who disagree with
them to be less trustworthy.

00:22:57.950 --> 00:23:00.143
Now, why would you do that?

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:03.260
So one idea here is

00:23:03.260 --> 00:23:08.260
that if you're evaluating
someone's information,

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:10.680
the information that
somebody is sharing with you

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:13.659
and you think, okay, this
is an epistemic process.

00:23:13.659 --> 00:23:17.760
They've somehow gathered this data.

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:20.410
I'd like to try to
evaluate how good they are

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:23.270
as epistemic agents, how
likely are they to come

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:27.193
to good conclusions, collect
data reliably and so on.

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:31.810
And I'm gonna use their
past epistemic success

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:34.433
by my own lights to evaluate that.

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:37.082
And so the idea is that

00:23:37.082 --> 00:23:40.070
if I look around and I
see, well, these people,

00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:41.940
according to the data
that I have access to

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:45.640
and the beliefs that I hold,
appear to have succeeded

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:47.170
in their epistemic tasks.

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:49.180
I'm gonna assume that
the data they're sharing

00:23:49.180 --> 00:23:53.050
with me is more reliable.

00:23:53.050 --> 00:23:55.070
And the people who by my own lights appear

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:57.510
to have failed at their epistemic tasks,

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:01.010
I'm gonna understand, do
likely be producing data

00:24:01.010 --> 00:24:02.253
that's less reliable.

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:05.240
Now, I'm still gonna treat that data

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:08.500
in general as something I can update on,

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:11.223
but I'm going to treat it as less certain.

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:13.373
So here's the idea.

00:24:14.223 --> 00:24:19.223
I am going to model trust here

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:26.260
as an assignment of uncertainty

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:30.930
to the amount, sorry,

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:33.580
assignment of uncertainty
to the data produced

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:38.270
by other agents where the amount

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:43.270
of uncertainty is described by
some function that decreases

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:47.183
with the distance between my beliefs

00:24:47.183 --> 00:24:50.393
and the agent who's
produced the data's beliefs.

00:24:52.059 --> 00:24:54.950
And so the idea here is that an agent who,

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:58.323
if someone produces evidence, that is,

00:24:58.323 --> 00:25:01.410
if someone holds beliefs that
are very distant from mine

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:03.870
whatever evidence they produced,
I'm going to treat that

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:08.820
as a more uncertain than evidence produced

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:12.180
by someone who is closer
to my own beliefs.

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:15.217
Now, the updating rule that
we use here, I didn't write it

00:25:15.217 --> 00:25:18.520
down on the slide, but it's
a standard updating rule,

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:23.130
an alternative to Bayes
rule for updating beliefs

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:26.630
in light of evidence known
as Jeffrey Conditionalization

00:25:26.630 --> 00:25:28.090
which is a standard way

00:25:28.090 --> 00:25:31.387
of updating beliefs in
light of uncertain evidence.

00:25:31.387 --> 00:25:32.740
And so what's going on here is

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:35.590
we're treating evidence produced by others

00:25:35.590 --> 00:25:39.090
as uncertain evidence with
some degree of uncertainty

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:42.140
that's determined by this function.

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:46.400
Now I'll just note that
this kind of strategy.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:50.630
So using Jeffrey
Conditionalization to model this,

00:25:50.630 --> 00:25:54.010
I believe his original to
this paper, but the basic kind

00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:58.423
of strategy where you
conditionalize, how you,

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:02.680
how agents update their
beliefs or their opinions

00:26:03.730 --> 00:26:08.730
based on some function of the
distance between their beliefs

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:14.240
or opinions is widespread
in the literature.

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:15.640
This is something that's been studied

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:17.110
at least since early 2000s

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:23.000
and so what we're doing is
adapting this kind of mechanism

00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:27.350
to the network of epistemology framework

00:26:27.350 --> 00:26:29.183
and introducing the idea

00:26:29.183 --> 00:26:33.573
that Jeffrey Conditionalization
is a useful way of modeling.

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:35.803
Now, I'll just note

00:26:35.803 --> 00:26:38.970
that we have to introduce
some decreasing function

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:42.200
of the distance between
agent's beliefs to do this.

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:45.060
We consider different ones,
just a linear function,

00:26:45.060 --> 00:26:50.060
exponential and some others,
and the qualitative results

00:26:51.990 --> 00:26:53.400
don't depend on that.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:55.940
As long as it's a strictly
decreasing function,

00:26:55.940 --> 00:27:00.250
we get qualitatively the same results

00:27:01.714 --> 00:27:04.230
and the qualitative results
are really what we take

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:09.180
to be trustworthy here
or in any way, you know,

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:10.830
the relevant output of the model.

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:14.490
Okay, so what are the results of this?

00:27:14.490 --> 00:27:16.743
Well, first there's a new possibility now.

00:27:18.034 --> 00:27:22.200
And the new possibility
is that for certain values

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:27.200
of this parameter M which
governs how strongly agents tend

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:35.763
to distrust other agents,

00:27:37.109 --> 00:27:40.330
we can begin to see polarization.

00:27:40.330 --> 00:27:45.330
So when that parameter
exceeds a certain value,

00:27:45.680 --> 00:27:49.410
conventionally set to one in
the model as we've set it up,

00:27:49.410 --> 00:27:52.220
we see polarization,
which are stable outcomes

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:55.680
where some agents have high
credence that B is better.

00:27:55.680 --> 00:27:58.360
And some have low
credence that B is better.

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:00.950
I.e. they believe that A is better

00:28:00.950 --> 00:28:02.450
and more over it's stable

00:28:02.450 --> 00:28:05.840
because the agents
don't trust one another.

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:09.660
They've come to occupy,
to be distanced enough

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:13.900
from one another in belief
space that the agents

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:18.583
that are performing action
B and producing evidence

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:22.323
that the better action is in fact better,

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:27.930
no longer influence the agents who believe

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:29.813
that agent, that action A is better.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:32.840
Right, and so what happens is

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:35.660
although lots of evidence
is being produced

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:38.310
and lots of evidence is being shared,

00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:41.820
you've gotten to a situation
where the agents are so distant

00:28:41.820 --> 00:28:44.150
from one another, that they
don't trust one another

00:28:44.150 --> 00:28:47.400
and they don't update
in light of the evidence

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:49.313
that's shared by the other group.

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:54.580
And one way of thinking
about what happens here is

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:58.770
as M becomes larger and
larger, the distance

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:05.840
in belief that needs to be
achieved in order for this,

00:29:06.193 --> 00:29:11.193
for failure of listening to
occur, gets smaller and smaller.

00:29:13.100 --> 00:29:15.120
And so the idea is that you know,

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:16.910
there's some parameter here

00:29:16.910 --> 00:29:18.810
that's going to govern how that works.

00:29:19.670 --> 00:29:21.286
And so here's a chart that showed

00:29:21.286 --> 00:29:23.453
how that happens in the model.

00:29:24.736 --> 00:29:27.860
As I said before, M equals
one is conventionally set

00:29:27.860 --> 00:29:31.530
as the value of the parameter
for which is even possible

00:29:31.530 --> 00:29:33.440
for them to stop listening
to one another, as opposed

00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:36.680
to merely treating each other's
evidence as very uncertain,

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:38.573
but still updating on it.

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:42.440
And so, as you see, as M gets larger,

00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:44.640
various sizes of networks.

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:49.553
What we see is that polarization
becomes increasingly likely

00:29:51.190 --> 00:29:54.770
and once M gets up to two
or a little bit higher,

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:59.723
you begin to see polarization
in all, or nearly all runs.

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:05.853
Okay, good.

00:30:06.810 --> 00:30:08.510
Just note that it's not just that,

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:12.980
this parameter M doesn't
just increase polarization,

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:16.820
it also increases the total number

00:30:16.820 --> 00:30:20.230
of false beliefs that are
held by the community.

00:30:20.230 --> 00:30:24.750
And so what you see here are
plots of average outcomes

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:28.530
across a large range of
different parameter values,

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:29.663
different values of N,

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:33.263
different values of the number
of agents in the network.

00:30:35.410 --> 00:30:36.980
And so on, different values of Epsilon,

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:39.650
how hard a problem it is,
how big is the difference

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:42.670
in expectation value between
action A and action B.

00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:44.250
And what you see is across all

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:47.403
of those different
parameters, as M increases,

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:52.140
the fraction of the network

00:30:53.540 --> 00:30:58.093
that ends up holding
false beliefs increases.

00:30:59.262 --> 00:31:02.550
And so you're gonna see
different fractions depending

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:03.640
on the different other parameters,

00:31:03.640 --> 00:31:07.533
but each of these lines is
higher than the one before it.

00:31:11.020 --> 00:31:14.750
Okay, so there's lots more that
I could say about this model

00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:16.530
but the point is to
describe several models.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:20.810
So let me move on and just
point to some of the takeaways

00:31:20.810 --> 00:31:23.400
from this model that are
going to be important

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.410
in discussion that follows.

00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:27.470
So I think the first takeaway is

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:30.070
just that polarization
can apparently occur

00:31:30.070 --> 00:31:32.060
under these sorts of circumstances.

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:34.990
That is, it can occur in
an epistemic community

00:31:34.990 --> 00:31:36.640
with certain kinds of properties

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:39.230
in which agents tend to
distrust evidence produced

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:44.230
by some agents where that
distrust is conditioned

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:46.823
on their difference in beliefs.

00:31:48.640 --> 00:31:53.640
Now in the present model,
the phenomenon is driven

00:31:54.360 --> 00:31:57.820
by the fact that agents explore,

00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:00.893
i.e. perform the action
that they think is better.

00:32:01.960 --> 00:32:04.210
And so it's the people
who believe B is better

00:32:04.210 --> 00:32:05.970
who are performing action B

00:32:06.950 --> 00:32:10.080
who are generating data about action B.

00:32:10.080 --> 00:32:14.080
And it's precisely those
agents that are ignored by

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:18.158
or distrusted by the ones
who hold the false belief.

00:32:18.158 --> 00:32:20.133
The ones who think that
action A is better.

00:32:21.051 --> 00:32:23.350
And so what's happening here is

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:28.350
that distrust is preventing
the reliable information

00:32:29.814 --> 00:32:34.490
from reaching the people who would need it

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:37.980
to reach more reliable beliefs.

00:32:39.757 --> 00:32:41.240
And so you might think of this

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:45.313
as possibly a way of
modeling certain kinds of,

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:50.440
certain kinds of vaccine hesitancy

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:53.810
where some people are,

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:57.480
believe that vaccines, let's
say a COVID vaccine is safe

00:32:57.480 --> 00:33:00.820
and effective, other people
think it hasn't been shown

00:33:00.820 --> 00:33:03.660
to be safe or it hasn't
been shown to be effective.

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:07.820
There's a lot of data now,
population level data,

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:09.720
hundreds of millions of
people across the world

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:11.430
who have received this vaccine.

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:15.500
Good data on what their
outcomes have been so far.

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:17.500
But if that data isn't trusted

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:20.040
by the people who are
skeptical for various reasons.

00:33:20.040 --> 00:33:21.250
Maybe they think it's manipulated.

00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:24.650
Maybe they think that people
are trying to mislead them

00:33:24.650 --> 00:33:26.400
or have some ulterior motive.

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:28.180
They're not going to update their beliefs

00:33:28.180 --> 00:33:31.010
in light of that data,
even though it's present.

00:33:31.010 --> 00:33:34.180
So I just wanna say once again,

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:36.200
this is a particular model, but in fact,

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:40.414
this is consonant with a very
large literature discussing

00:33:40.414 --> 00:33:44.380
especially what's sometimes
known as opinion dynamics

00:33:45.550 --> 00:33:50.550
in the case where agents
condition how their beliefs change

00:33:51.659 --> 00:33:54.520
on the distance between their belief

00:33:54.520 --> 00:33:57.033
or their opinion and that of other agents.

00:33:58.020 --> 00:33:59.960
So this is a widely studied phenomenon.

00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:04.180
It's one common possible
explanation that you see

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:08.110
in the mathematical
social sciences literature

00:34:08.110 --> 00:34:10.313
for how polarization might arise.

00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:13.820
Okay, now I'm gonna switch gears.

00:34:13.820 --> 00:34:16.360
We're gonna talk about another
network epistemology model.

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:18.860
One that invokes a different mechanism

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:22.963
to explain polarization which
I'm gonna call conformity.

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:25.060
Once again, this is based

00:34:25.060 --> 00:34:28.003
on a paper that Cailin
and I wrote together.

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:32.850
Cailin doesn't like this paper as much as,

00:34:32.850 --> 00:34:34.950
so she let me be first author on this one.

00:34:36.580 --> 00:34:38.940
Okay, so conformity.

00:34:38.940 --> 00:34:42.535
So there's a very long literature
in social psychology that,

00:34:42.535 --> 00:34:47.030
experimental literature
that documents a tendency

00:34:47.030 --> 00:34:50.180
of human subjects to conform

00:34:50.180 --> 00:34:52.403
with members of their social group.

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:56.040
The Asch experiments in particular is one

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:58.610
where you have a subject

00:34:59.930 --> 00:35:02.530
in a group where the other groups are

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:03.930
not experimental subjects,

00:35:03.930 --> 00:35:07.893
but are members of the research team.

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:12.130
And what happens is the
whole group are shown

00:35:12.130 --> 00:35:13.700
a number of pictures, right?

00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:14.720
So you have a line

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:19.720
and then two other lines with
two or three other lines.

00:35:20.533 --> 00:35:21.660
Anyway, you're shown a line,
you're shown some other lines

00:35:21.660 --> 00:35:24.580
and you're asked, is this
first line the same length

00:35:24.580 --> 00:35:26.700
as one of these other ones.

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:31.520
And the various research
group participants,

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:35.710
I mean, they're the ones
who are not subjects say

00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:39.440
that something that's obviously false.

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:42.350
That, you know, this line which
is clearly the same length

00:35:42.350 --> 00:35:43.800
as this other line is longer.

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:48.330
And then the research subject is asked

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:52.730
to give their opinion on this.

00:35:52.730 --> 00:35:57.730
And you find that in a
significant number of cases,

00:35:57.780 --> 00:36:02.447
they will go along with
the group, despite the fact

00:36:03.300 --> 00:36:05.670
that just the evidence
of their senses, right?

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:08.390
The fact that it's obviously the case,

00:36:08.390 --> 00:36:12.350
that the group has, you
know, the other members

00:36:12.350 --> 00:36:15.533
of the group have
expressed some false view.

00:36:17.650 --> 00:36:19.700
Okay, so given that tendency,

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:22.500
we may try to introduce conformity

00:36:22.500 --> 00:36:26.770
into a network of epistemology
framework as follows.

00:36:26.770 --> 00:36:30.460
So the idea here is that
when you're making a decision

00:36:30.460 --> 00:36:32.223
about what action to perform,

00:36:33.252 --> 00:36:36.670
you might be thinking
about the payoff you'll get

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:38.070
from performing that action,

00:36:39.254 --> 00:36:41.710
but you might also be considering
some other preferences

00:36:41.710 --> 00:36:43.057
that will enter

00:36:43.057 --> 00:36:44.910
into an expected value calculation.

00:36:44.910 --> 00:36:49.840
And so one such preference
might be a desire

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:54.583
to conform your actions to
the actions of your neighbors.

00:36:56.020 --> 00:37:01.020
So we model this by adding
a conformity parameter

00:37:01.780 --> 00:37:05.270
which tracks how much payoff actors get,

00:37:05.270 --> 00:37:08.300
simply from matching their behaviors

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:10.880
to those of their network neighbors.

00:37:10.880 --> 00:37:15.880
Now, we once again adopt a
very low rationality model here

00:37:15.950 --> 00:37:19.430
where we aren't you know,
each agent isn't trying

00:37:19.430 --> 00:37:20.800
to model the beliefs

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:23.070
or likely actions of the
other agents around them.

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:24.560
All they're doing is they're saying, well,

00:37:24.560 --> 00:37:26.410
what did people do in the last round?

00:37:27.380 --> 00:37:32.380
If most people did action
A, then I'm going to expect

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:37.630
to get a payoff from
conforming to action A

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:40.350
and in order to choose to do action B,

00:37:40.350 --> 00:37:43.330
I need to not only believe
that action B is better,

00:37:43.330 --> 00:37:45.430
but I also need it to be the case

00:37:45.430 --> 00:37:50.430
that the benefits that I get by, you know,

00:37:50.450 --> 00:37:51.950
the good feeling I get

00:37:51.950 --> 00:37:56.010
by conforming all of my
neighbors doing action A has

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:59.163
to overcome the benefits
of performing action B.

00:38:00.750 --> 00:38:02.720
And so the idea here is that we're going

00:38:02.720 --> 00:38:04.570
to weigh two different desires.

00:38:04.570 --> 00:38:06.400
A desire to choose accurate actions

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:09.430
and get the payoffs that come with that

00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:11.853
and a desire to conform with neighbors.

00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:18.150
Now, when we introduce
this, we once again,

00:38:18.150 --> 00:38:20.890
like with the trust dynamics,

00:38:20.890 --> 00:38:24.210
begin to see new possible
outcomes for the model.

00:38:24.210 --> 00:38:27.310
So simulations can still
approach either true consensus

00:38:27.310 --> 00:38:28.143
or false consensus.

00:38:28.143 --> 00:38:30.040
Those are still possible outcomes,

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:33.240
but another possible
outcome that doesn't exist

00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:35.360
in the base model is a situation

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:38.500
where the network may
converge to a situation

00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:40.503
where everyone performs the same action,

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:45.600
even though some agents secretly believe

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:47.333
that the other action is better,

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:50.190
but they don't perform the other action

00:38:51.353 --> 00:38:55.950
because they prefer to conform.

00:38:55.950 --> 00:38:58.120
And because they don't
perform the other action,

00:38:58.120 --> 00:39:00.450
no new evidence regarding the superiority

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:03.853
of the other action is
introduced into the network.

00:39:04.900 --> 00:39:09.010
And so, although there are,

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:12.090
there's some diversity of
belief here, it's not reflected

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:15.253
in the data that's generated
and shared in the network.

00:39:16.768 --> 00:39:21.163
And we call that situation.

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:23.020
We have two different names,

00:39:23.020 --> 00:39:25.060
one is true disagreement
and false disagreements.

00:39:25.060 --> 00:39:26.470
So true disagreement is a situation

00:39:26.470 --> 00:39:28.510
where everyone's performing
the better action,

00:39:28.510 --> 00:39:30.020
but there's disagreement still.

00:39:30.020 --> 00:39:31.410
The other situation is

00:39:31.410 --> 00:39:34.020
where everyone's performing
the worst action,

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:37.840
performing action A, even
though some people believe

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:39.040
that action B is better.

00:39:40.660 --> 00:39:43.150
Now finally, we can find an outcome

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:47.190
where you get polarization
in action and beliefs.

00:39:47.190 --> 00:39:51.850
So the idea here is that
under some circumstances,

00:39:51.850 --> 00:39:55.850
specifically in the presence
of certain network structures,

00:39:55.850 --> 00:40:00.327
what can happen is that
one group comes to believe

00:40:02.010 --> 00:40:05.960
that action A is better, and
they all perform action A.

00:40:05.960 --> 00:40:09.990
And another group comes to
believe that action B is better

00:40:09.990 --> 00:40:12.370
and they all perform action B,

00:40:12.370 --> 00:40:15.970
but the information
doesn't pass effectively

00:40:15.970 --> 00:40:17.113
between the groups.

00:40:17.990 --> 00:40:20.370
So to see how this depends
on network structure,

00:40:20.370 --> 00:40:24.350
let me introduce a few
basic network structures.

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:27.512
So consider lots of different things.

00:40:27.512 --> 00:40:32.120
The cycle, the wheel,
the complete network,

00:40:32.120 --> 00:40:34.440
these are standard networks to consider.

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:35.310
They're not that important

00:40:35.310 --> 00:40:36.750
for our purposes, the
ones that are important

00:40:36.750 --> 00:40:38.070
for our purposes are what happens

00:40:38.070 --> 00:40:40.320
when you have a randomly generated network

00:40:40.320 --> 00:40:41.450
using various different kinds

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:45.120
of algorithms for generating
networks, or in particular,

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:46.100
you look at networks

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:50.930
that you might describe as
clumpy or having cliques.

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:54.100
So the idea here is that
polarization is going

00:40:54.100 --> 00:40:56.180
to appear specifically

00:40:56.180 --> 00:41:01.180
in cases where networks
have a clique structure.

00:41:02.320 --> 00:41:03.850
So how does this work?

00:41:03.850 --> 00:41:06.120
Well, the idea is if you have,

00:41:06.120 --> 00:41:08.410
a clique here is defined as a group

00:41:08.410 --> 00:41:11.619
of agents that are tightly
connected to one another,

00:41:11.619 --> 00:41:15.800
but only weakly connected
to other groups of agents

00:41:15.800 --> 00:41:19.210
who are in turn mutually tightly
connected to one another.

00:41:19.210 --> 00:41:21.330
As we see in this case,
we have two separate,

00:41:21.330 --> 00:41:24.583
complete networks that are
linked only by a single edge.

00:41:26.236 --> 00:41:28.820
And so the way in which
polarization can appear here is

00:41:28.820 --> 00:41:30.493
that in one of these cliques,

00:41:31.390 --> 00:41:34.970
everyone comes to believe
actually B is better.

00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:38.320
They perform action B and they,

00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:42.760
that sort of mini network
approach as a true consensus.

00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:44.310
Whereas in the other clique,

00:41:44.310 --> 00:41:46.590
everyone comes to perform action A

00:41:47.720 --> 00:41:51.070
and almost all of them believe
that action A is better.

00:41:51.070 --> 00:41:56.070
But in fact, the one agent in
this picture who has access

00:41:56.440 --> 00:41:59.810
to the information from the
other clique will generally

00:41:59.810 --> 00:42:02.730
over time, come to update
on that information,

00:42:02.730 --> 00:42:05.180
come to believe that in
fact, action B is better,

00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:08.950
but because of their
preference to conform,

00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:11.720
and there's only one action B doer

00:42:11.720 --> 00:42:13.633
in their immediate network,

00:42:13.633 --> 00:42:14.630
but there are many action A doers

00:42:14.630 --> 00:42:16.080
in their immediate network.

00:42:16.080 --> 00:42:18.570
They never switched their action,

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:22.430
but because they're a kind of
bridge for this information,

00:42:22.430 --> 00:42:24.160
because they never switched their action,

00:42:24.160 --> 00:42:28.100
the other agents in their clique
never received data showing

00:42:28.100 --> 00:42:29.973
that action B is better.

00:42:30.970 --> 00:42:35.837
So the picture is one
where that agent conforming

00:42:35.837 --> 00:42:38.050
sort of blocks the spread

00:42:38.050 --> 00:42:40.743
of reliable information into their clique.

00:42:42.910 --> 00:42:47.780
And so you can see different
kinds of outcomes here

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:50.650
depending on how strong this
conformity parameter is.

00:42:50.650 --> 00:42:52.300
Basically kind of informative parameter is

00:42:52.300 --> 00:42:56.410
just a measure of how
strong the preference is

00:42:56.410 --> 00:43:00.440
for conforming your actions
to those of your neighbors.

00:43:00.440 --> 00:43:04.970
What'd you see is that, you know,

00:43:04.970 --> 00:43:08.630
as conformity increases,
correct consensus still tends

00:43:08.630 --> 00:43:10.913
to be more likely.

00:43:12.177 --> 00:43:17.177
Correct disagreement is the
next most likely outcome.

00:43:17.520 --> 00:43:20.740
And then you start seeing
these incorrect consensus

00:43:20.740 --> 00:43:22.120
and incorrect disagreements,

00:43:22.120 --> 00:43:24.832
but then you have this
very small likelihood here

00:43:24.832 --> 00:43:27.810
of getting polarization,
but that, you know,

00:43:27.810 --> 00:43:29.190
how big that likelihood is

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:32.418
depends on just what your
network structure is.

00:43:32.418 --> 00:43:33.458
And so if we look

00:43:33.458 --> 00:43:34.900
at specifically different
network structures,

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:36.840
we see different results.

00:43:36.840 --> 00:43:41.672
So especially in the
case of high conformity,

00:43:41.672 --> 00:43:44.650
which is the plot on the left
here, unless I'm mirrored,

00:43:44.650 --> 00:43:46.907
in which case it's the part on the right,

00:43:46.907 --> 00:43:49.730
the one that's labeled high conformity.

00:43:49.730 --> 00:43:52.610
What you see is that in
the clumpy case, right?

00:43:52.610 --> 00:43:55.560
This case with the
strong clique structure,

00:43:55.560 --> 00:43:59.113
nearly half of all outcomes
are polarized outcomes.

00:44:00.070 --> 00:44:04.140
And so if you have a network
that has that structure,

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:08.450
then you can expect
polarization to occur simply due

00:44:08.450 --> 00:44:11.883
to conformity without needing
any other explanation.

00:44:14.590 --> 00:44:16.420
So what can we take away from this?

00:44:16.420 --> 00:44:19.703
Well, in the presence of
certain network structure,

00:44:20.630 --> 00:44:22.940
conformity provides
another sufficient cause

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:24.380
for polarization.

00:44:24.380 --> 00:44:26.150
And that's because of this mechanism

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:29.090
whereby you get
bottlenecks, as I described.

00:44:29.090 --> 00:44:30.160
Now, if you're thinking,

00:44:30.160 --> 00:44:33.290
well I mean, how realistic are these sorts

00:44:33.290 --> 00:44:35.930
of clumpy networks?

00:44:35.930 --> 00:44:38.167
Well, the answer is
that in fact, research,

00:44:41.130 --> 00:44:43.750
empirical research on all sorts

00:44:43.750 --> 00:44:45.680
of real human social networks

00:44:45.680 --> 00:44:49.690
from the structure of Facebook networks

00:44:49.690 --> 00:44:54.690
to studies of sort of ordinary
offline populations suggest

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:58.300
that real social networks
have a characteristic known

00:44:58.300 --> 00:45:03.140
as a small world property,
which means that agents tend

00:45:05.590 --> 00:45:10.590
to have few connections, but
to have a very short pathway

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:12.910
between them and anyone
else is sometimes called

00:45:12.910 --> 00:45:14.173
the Kevin Bacon effect.

00:45:17.483 --> 00:45:20.810
- James, are these also
referred to as bubbles?

00:45:20.810 --> 00:45:22.910
Or is that slightly different?

00:45:22.910 --> 00:45:24.790
- So that's slightly different.

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:29.790
This predates the idea of
filter bubbles by two decades.

00:45:31.770 --> 00:45:34.950
Yeah, so this is just a general
result in network theory

00:45:34.950 --> 00:45:36.760
that you can describe small world networks

00:45:36.760 --> 00:45:38.290
and then it turns out
to be an empirical fact

00:45:38.290 --> 00:45:40.126
that you have these sorts

00:45:40.126 --> 00:45:42.980
of small world networks
in real situations where,

00:45:42.980 --> 00:45:43.940
and they're called small worlds

00:45:43.940 --> 00:45:46.890
because the small world
metaphor isn't supposed

00:45:46.890 --> 00:45:51.500
to be one where you think we
have these isolated worlds

00:45:51.500 --> 00:45:52.680
that are distinct from one another,

00:45:52.680 --> 00:45:55.460
like little worlds, rather it's
the small world phenomenon.

00:45:55.460 --> 00:45:58.483
Like who would have known
that you knew so, and so,

00:46:00.025 --> 00:46:01.088
or that you were just two

00:46:01.088 --> 00:46:02.690
or three connections away from so-and-so.

00:46:02.690 --> 00:46:04.370
Now, I don't remember the number exactly,

00:46:04.370 --> 00:46:07.380
but it's something like on
average, the average path lines

00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:10.323
between random Facebook members is a very,

00:46:14.790 --> 00:46:16.473
very low, it's like four or five.

00:46:17.870 --> 00:46:19.304
And so, you know,

00:46:19.304 --> 00:46:21.260
that the average path length
for information to spread

00:46:21.260 --> 00:46:24.713
to anywhere in the network
is surprisingly short.

00:46:25.870 --> 00:46:26.703
But the point is

00:46:26.703 --> 00:46:29.565
that these small world networks
are ones that have cliques.

00:46:29.565 --> 00:46:34.565
And if you, there's a standard algorithm

00:46:34.630 --> 00:46:36.110
for generating small world networks,

00:46:36.110 --> 00:46:37.890
generating random small world networks

00:46:37.890 --> 00:46:39.950
and I'm just the Strogatz-Watts algorithm.

00:46:39.950 --> 00:46:42.200
You use that algorithm
to generate your networks

00:46:42.200 --> 00:46:44.790
and using random networks for
these conformity dynamics.

00:46:44.790 --> 00:46:49.640
You find polarization
at a much higher rate

00:46:49.640 --> 00:46:52.580
than in the other sort
of more uniform networks

00:46:52.580 --> 00:46:54.283
that I introduced.

00:46:56.369 --> 00:46:58.824
Okay, so this is another kind

00:46:58.824 --> 00:47:02.730
of possible explanation for
polarization having to do

00:47:02.730 --> 00:47:05.763
with the network structure
of human interaction.

00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:09.670
Let me now introduce yet another model

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:13.533
with a totally different
framework and mechanism.

00:47:14.830 --> 00:47:18.040
Now this last paper is a work in progress.

00:47:18.040 --> 00:47:20.040
It's collaborative with Cailin, but also

00:47:20.040 --> 00:47:24.043
with one of our graduate
students, Aydin Mohseni.

00:47:27.140 --> 00:47:31.950
Okay, so the last models
considered social factors

00:47:33.050 --> 00:47:37.480
as a ways in which people update

00:47:37.480 --> 00:47:42.480
on evidence from other agents
and change their behavior

00:47:42.890 --> 00:47:45.240
in light of the behavior of other agents

00:47:45.240 --> 00:47:47.273
to explain how polarization can appear.

00:47:48.230 --> 00:47:51.730
In this model, we're going
to look just at individuals

00:47:51.730 --> 00:47:56.680
who are engaged in kind of
a Bayesian learning activity

00:47:59.750 --> 00:48:04.750
based on a stream of data
generated by some outside source.

00:48:05.760 --> 00:48:08.230
And we're gonna understand
this data generated

00:48:08.230 --> 00:48:10.810
by the outside source
to represent information

00:48:10.810 --> 00:48:14.190
that they receive from some media.

00:48:14.190 --> 00:48:16.720
So you can think about this

00:48:16.720 --> 00:48:18.370
as applying to different kinds of cases.

00:48:18.370 --> 00:48:21.197
You can think about it
as applying to, you know,

00:48:22.380 --> 00:48:23.900
traditional news media.

00:48:23.900 --> 00:48:25.917
You could think about it as
applying to social media.

00:48:25.917 --> 00:48:29.500
But the idea is that there's some process

00:48:29.500 --> 00:48:33.090
by which the data that
they see is generated

00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:34.230
by some intermediary.

00:48:34.230 --> 00:48:36.840
That the data that they
see is somehow curated

00:48:36.840 --> 00:48:41.060
by something between them and the world.

00:48:41.060 --> 00:48:43.570
And the idea is that
there's a curation process

00:48:43.570 --> 00:48:46.410
that involves some practice use, right?

00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:50.470
So some processes involved
in choosing what data

00:48:50.470 --> 00:48:52.610
of all the data that's sort of generated

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:56.526
in the world agents actually update on.

00:48:56.526 --> 00:49:00.750
What we're gonna ask is how
those practices interact

00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:04.383
with individual reasoning
biases to influence belies.

00:49:07.400 --> 00:49:09.843
So here's the basic picture.

00:49:10.890 --> 00:49:14.400
These media do not report
on all events in a domain.

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:16.960
Some events are selected for coverage.

00:49:16.960 --> 00:49:20.340
So how do you choose
which events to cover?

00:49:20.340 --> 00:49:23.640
Now, if you look at actual practice,

00:49:23.640 --> 00:49:27.560
you look at norms for how
journalists should practice,

00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:32.560
you see various criteria
described that are applied

00:49:32.780 --> 00:49:36.573
to select which of the events
are gonna be reported on.

00:49:37.782 --> 00:49:38.615
And so, just as an example,

00:49:38.615 --> 00:49:42.530
it's a classic adage of a journalism.

00:49:42.530 --> 00:49:45.030
You don't report on dog bites man,

00:49:45.030 --> 00:49:46.720
you report on man bites dog, right?

00:49:46.720 --> 00:49:49.440
So you report on the things
that are unusual or unexpected

00:49:49.440 --> 00:49:51.900
or perhaps extreme events, right?

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:54.170
The biggest hurricane, you know,

00:49:54.170 --> 00:49:57.400
the worst hurricane season,
you know, you don't report

00:49:57.400 --> 00:49:59.360
on every earthquake, you
report on the ones that are

00:49:59.360 --> 00:50:02.663
above a certain threshold
on the Richter scale.

00:50:03.818 --> 00:50:08.650
Another example of a criteria
that might be applied.

00:50:08.650 --> 00:50:11.260
The long-standing norm in journalism,

00:50:11.260 --> 00:50:16.130
which in the US was actually
part of a official FCC policy

00:50:16.130 --> 00:50:20.440
until the 1980s, that enjoins journalists

00:50:20.440 --> 00:50:24.760
previously required
journalists to report fairly.

00:50:24.760 --> 00:50:28.210
Now, this idea of a
fair reporting was given

00:50:28.210 --> 00:50:31.270
a particular characterization.

00:50:31.270 --> 00:50:32.240
The idea was that

00:50:33.130 --> 00:50:36.293
if you ever reported on
some controversial matter,

00:50:37.970 --> 00:50:40.030
you would need either within your story

00:50:40.030 --> 00:50:43.800
or elsewhere within your organization

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:45.850
to also give equal time

00:50:45.850 --> 00:50:48.283
to other sides of that issue.

00:50:49.220 --> 00:50:52.960
So even to this day,
USA today for instance,

00:50:52.960 --> 00:50:56.950
they run an editorial on
a controversial topic,

00:50:56.950 --> 00:50:57.900
they always pair it

00:50:57.900 --> 00:51:01.860
with an editorial supporting
the opposite position.

00:51:01.860 --> 00:51:06.100
When you also see within
just regular articles,

00:51:06.100 --> 00:51:09.070
an expectation that the
other side will be described

00:51:09.070 --> 00:51:13.893
or will be given some, some
space to express their views.

00:51:15.150 --> 00:51:17.350
Okay, so these are two different examples

00:51:17.350 --> 00:51:20.420
of what I mean by criteria
that may be applied

00:51:20.420 --> 00:51:22.463
to select which events are reported.

00:51:24.160 --> 00:51:25.780
So how are we going to model this?

00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:26.670
Well, here's the idea.

00:51:26.670 --> 00:51:31.670
The idea is that there's some range

00:51:32.100 --> 00:51:34.723
of events that occur in the world.

00:51:35.630 --> 00:51:38.260
Think of it, maybe as storms

00:51:39.280 --> 00:51:44.280
and the storms are sampled from
some normal distribution, W.

00:51:44.778 --> 00:51:46.850
So you can think of the
severity of the storm

00:51:46.850 --> 00:51:51.680
as just being something
that is randomly generated,

00:51:51.680 --> 00:51:54.370
sampled from some random
variable distributed

00:51:54.370 --> 00:51:55.730
according to this normal distribution.

00:51:55.730 --> 00:51:59.453
W with fixed mean and
fixed standard deviation.

00:52:00.790 --> 00:52:03.060
Now journalists aren't gonna
cover every storm that happens.

00:52:03.060 --> 00:52:04.640
What they're gonna do is
they're going to select

00:52:04.640 --> 00:52:07.410
from this initial distribution,
which events to cover

00:52:08.619 --> 00:52:09.452
and you can think of this

00:52:09.452 --> 00:52:14.330
as a process by which the
distribution W is transformed

00:52:14.330 --> 00:52:15.790
into another distribution,

00:52:15.790 --> 00:52:18.940
we to call a reported distribution, R

00:52:18.940 --> 00:52:20.673
which generally differs from W.

00:52:22.310 --> 00:52:25.130
Okay, and so now we have some
agents who are interested

00:52:25.130 --> 00:52:27.370
in learning about how the world is,

00:52:27.370 --> 00:52:29.320
but they're getting their
information from journalists.

00:52:29.320 --> 00:52:32.700
And so what they're doing is
they're updating their beliefs

00:52:32.700 --> 00:52:35.660
on data that's randomly sampled
from this new distribution

00:52:35.660 --> 00:52:38.563
or the distribution of reported events.

00:52:40.221 --> 00:52:41.319
And what we're interested in is

00:52:41.319 --> 00:52:44.690
how does their posterior distribution,

00:52:44.690 --> 00:52:49.690
how does the Bayesian agent's
beliefs about the world differ

00:52:50.530 --> 00:52:52.797
from the distribution of actual events?

00:52:52.797 --> 00:52:54.580
And in particular,

00:52:54.580 --> 00:52:57.080
we're going to assume that
the underlying process,

00:52:57.080 --> 00:52:58.410
we can take the agents to assume

00:52:58.410 --> 00:53:00.950
that the underlying processes
IS Gaussian with unknown mean

00:53:00.950 --> 00:53:03.610
and variance, they're going
to assume interchangeability

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:06.500
and what we're going to
is model their beliefs

00:53:06.500 --> 00:53:09.350
with what's known as a normal
inverse gamma distribution.

00:53:09.350 --> 00:53:11.060
Now, the important thing here is just,

00:53:11.060 --> 00:53:15.730
that distribution allows them
to calculate an expected value

00:53:15.730 --> 00:53:18.160
for the mean of W

00:53:18.160 --> 00:53:21.270
and an expected value for
the standard deviation

00:53:21.270 --> 00:53:22.980
or the variance of W

00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:25.730
and what's gonna happen
is we're gonna be able

00:53:25.730 --> 00:53:28.127
to compare those expected values

00:53:28.127 --> 00:53:29.780
for the mean, and the variance

00:53:29.780 --> 00:53:31.793
with the actual mean and variance of W.

00:53:34.330 --> 00:53:37.040
Okay, and so we're gonna assume

00:53:37.040 --> 00:53:39.210
that W is some normal distribution.

00:53:39.210 --> 00:53:41.880
It's got some fixed mean and variance.

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:46.264
The mean mew in general can be off zero.

00:53:46.264 --> 00:53:47.097
And so we're gonna assume

00:53:47.097 --> 00:53:48.560
that there's some social neutral points.

00:53:48.560 --> 00:53:53.560
Sort of what, you know,
what people take, you know,

00:53:55.226 --> 00:53:57.020
what journalists I should say,

00:53:57.020 --> 00:54:01.490
take to be the sort of common
knowledge about, you know,

00:54:01.490 --> 00:54:03.080
where things are on average,

00:54:03.080 --> 00:54:05.230
but the actual distribution
is going to be,

00:54:06.210 --> 00:54:08.703
maybe deviate from that.

00:54:10.060 --> 00:54:12.280
And now we're gonna consider
a few different processes

00:54:12.280 --> 00:54:15.163
by which the reported
distribution R, is constructed.

00:54:16.090 --> 00:54:20.650
So one hyperbole,
journalists just exaggerate.

00:54:20.650 --> 00:54:25.650
So sometimes, so what happens
is, you know, X occurs.

00:54:25.710 --> 00:54:29.063
They report some factor
H times X, you know,

00:54:30.167 --> 00:54:33.150
it's the biggest storm,
the worst hurricane,

00:54:33.150 --> 00:54:36.733
the biggest earthquake, even
if it isn't really the biggest.

00:54:37.969 --> 00:54:40.320
Okay, and so we have some parameter H

00:54:40.320 --> 00:54:41.720
that governs how that works.

00:54:42.650 --> 00:54:46.800
Here's another process, extremity.

00:54:46.800 --> 00:54:47.860
Journalists only report

00:54:47.860 --> 00:54:51.653
on events that lies some
distance from the neutral point.

00:54:53.210 --> 00:54:55.260
So this is again,

00:54:55.260 --> 00:54:57.650
like I'm not gonna report
on every earthquake.

00:54:57.650 --> 00:54:59.100
I'm only going to report on the ones

00:54:59.100 --> 00:55:02.283
that exceed some value
on the Richter scale.

00:55:03.960 --> 00:55:06.070
And then the final one is fair reporting

00:55:06.070 --> 00:55:07.430
which I discussed before?

00:55:07.430 --> 00:55:09.350
And the way that we're
gonna model that here is

00:55:09.350 --> 00:55:11.250
that every time you have an event

00:55:11.250 --> 00:55:12.530
that journalists wanna report on,

00:55:12.530 --> 00:55:14.920
on one side of the neutral point,

00:55:14.920 --> 00:55:17.323
they need to look for another event

00:55:17.323 --> 00:55:19.970
that represents the other side.

00:55:19.970 --> 00:55:23.730
So every time they report on one side,

00:55:23.730 --> 00:55:25.920
they report on the other side as well.

00:55:25.920 --> 00:55:28.053
Now they're always reporting real events.

00:55:28.910 --> 00:55:33.770
They're just pairing them
in a way where every event

00:55:33.770 --> 00:55:36.253
on one side corresponds
to an event on the other.

00:55:37.770 --> 00:55:40.840
Now we're also, we also consider
distributions that result

00:55:40.840 --> 00:55:42.665
from combining these.

00:55:42.665 --> 00:55:43.768
We'll look at each of these individually,

00:55:43.768 --> 00:55:45.830
we combine them in various ways.

00:55:45.830 --> 00:55:49.010
Now, just to give you a
sense of what this does.

00:55:49.010 --> 00:55:53.230
Each of these transformations
can be understood

00:55:53.230 --> 00:55:55.550
to produce a distribution.

00:55:55.550 --> 00:55:59.820
And so if we start with
our distribution W,

00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:02.200
labeled here as the
objective distribution,

00:56:02.200 --> 00:56:06.080
what hyperbole does is sort
of flatten that distribution,

00:56:06.080 --> 00:56:08.070
but also shift its mean a little bit away

00:56:08.070 --> 00:56:11.393
from the neutral point, zero.

00:56:12.790 --> 00:56:14.810
What extremity bias does is it

00:56:14.810 --> 00:56:18.850
just cuts out part of the events.

00:56:18.850 --> 00:56:21.770
So not all of the events
are reported, just the tails

00:56:23.320 --> 00:56:27.870
and then fair reporting does this thing

00:56:27.870 --> 00:56:32.870
where you basically take
the part of the distribution

00:56:33.070 --> 00:56:38.070
on each side of the neutral point

00:56:38.080 --> 00:56:42.440
and you normalize it
so that the probability

00:56:42.440 --> 00:56:44.803
of being on one side
or on the other is 0.5,

00:56:46.587 --> 00:56:48.787
or that corresponds to
pairing these events.

00:56:49.684 --> 00:56:54.360
But you do it in such
a way that you're only,

00:56:54.360 --> 00:56:55.330
you're keeping the shape

00:56:55.330 --> 00:56:58.690
of the distribution the
same, you're just rescaling

00:56:58.690 --> 00:57:02.280
so that the total area
underneath the distribution,

00:57:02.280 --> 00:57:05.180
to the left of zero is equal
to that to the right of zero.

00:57:07.247 --> 00:57:09.100
So what sorts of results do we see here?

00:57:09.100 --> 00:57:12.550
Well, an agent updating on
data sample from R convergences

00:57:12.550 --> 00:57:15.213
to a posterior distribution
that approximates R.

00:57:16.250 --> 00:57:18.440
So we're just approximating
each of these distributions

00:57:18.440 --> 00:57:20.100
as a normal distribution.

00:57:20.100 --> 00:57:21.446
On generally,

00:57:21.446 --> 00:57:23.960
these are gonna differ
from the true distribution.

00:57:23.960 --> 00:57:26.500
You can vat your error
with respect to the mean

00:57:26.500 --> 00:57:28.950
or the variance, but you're gonna be able

00:57:28.950 --> 00:57:33.950
to see these as a pretty
straightforward approximation

00:57:34.170 --> 00:57:38.360
of the reported evidence.

00:57:38.360 --> 00:57:39.920
So what you see then is that, well,

00:57:39.920 --> 00:57:42.490
the way you get the most
reliable beliefs is just

00:57:42.490 --> 00:57:45.510
by reporting on the true
distribution of evidence.

00:57:45.510 --> 00:57:47.323
So here's just an example of this.

00:57:49.128 --> 00:57:51.260
I'm sort of cognizant of time here.

00:57:51.260 --> 00:57:52.150
I'm timing myself.

00:57:52.150 --> 00:57:54.522
I've been going for 52 minutes,

00:57:54.522 --> 00:57:55.355
but I started a little bit late

00:57:55.355 --> 00:57:56.188
and I know that it's one now.

00:57:56.188 --> 00:57:58.823
So I'm going to pick up
the pace a little bit here.

00:58:00.460 --> 00:58:03.920
So here's an example of this.

00:58:03.920 --> 00:58:05.810
You have some true
distribution of evidence.

00:58:05.810 --> 00:58:08.380
You then have a reported
distribution of events,

00:58:08.380 --> 00:58:12.430
combining some of these
different distortions.

00:58:12.430 --> 00:58:15.683
And then you end up with some posterior.

00:58:16.600 --> 00:58:20.240
This is the one on the, pink one here,

00:58:20.240 --> 00:58:24.480
some posterior distribution
that approximates

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:27.303
with the normal distribution,
the reported evidence.

00:58:29.570 --> 00:58:34.570
Okay, so now let's consider
the following addition

00:58:35.880 --> 00:58:37.370
to this model.

00:58:37.370 --> 00:58:39.500
We suppose that instead of updating

00:58:39.500 --> 00:58:42.273
on all of the data that
journalists produce,

00:58:43.800 --> 00:58:47.910
agents have a cognitive bias.

00:58:47.910 --> 00:58:52.910
They have a tendency to prefer to update

00:58:53.410 --> 00:58:58.410
on data that is consistent
with their existing beliefs.

00:59:00.970 --> 00:59:04.250
So this is known as confirmation bias,

00:59:04.250 --> 00:59:07.150
where individuals seek out evidence

00:59:07.150 --> 00:59:09.266
that supports their existing beliefs,

00:59:09.266 --> 00:59:11.500
and they ignore evidence
that conflicts with them,

00:59:11.500 --> 00:59:14.560
or alternatively sometimes
they re-interpret evidence

00:59:14.560 --> 00:59:16.370
that conflicts with their beliefs

00:59:16.370 --> 00:59:19.913
as supporting their
beliefs when it doesn't.

00:59:21.230 --> 00:59:23.570
So here's how we're gonna
implement this idea.

00:59:23.570 --> 00:59:25.620
We're gonna suppose that
there's some probability

00:59:25.620 --> 00:59:29.420
for any reported events
that the agent updates

00:59:29.420 --> 00:59:30.253
on that event,

00:59:31.510 --> 00:59:34.270
but that probability is
gonna be less than one.

00:59:34.270 --> 00:59:36.788
And it's going to be a decreasing function

00:59:36.788 --> 00:59:39.280
of the distance between data

00:59:40.200 --> 00:59:43.320
and the agent's expectations
for the mean result.

00:59:43.320 --> 00:59:46.690
So, one way of thinking
about this is the probability

00:59:46.690 --> 00:59:49.230
of updating on data

00:59:49.230 --> 00:59:53.960
that the agent thinks is very
likely is going to be higher

00:59:53.960 --> 00:59:56.000
than the probability of updating

00:59:56.000 --> 00:59:59.073
on data that the agent
thinks is very unlikely.

01:00:01.970 --> 01:00:05.000
So what happens when we
introduced this modification?

01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:10.000
Well, it turns out then with
this modification, once again,

01:00:10.740 --> 01:00:14.520
based on very well-documented
psychological behavior

01:00:14.520 --> 01:00:18.510
and real people, we get a very
different kind of outcome.

01:00:18.510 --> 01:00:20.708
Now, what happens is instead

01:00:20.708 --> 01:00:24.560
of trying to approximate
the whole distribution

01:00:24.560 --> 01:00:27.010
of reported events with the Gaussian,

01:00:27.010 --> 01:00:30.490
what we find is that
agents tend to converge

01:00:30.490 --> 01:00:35.290
to a Gaussian centered at or near one

01:00:35.290 --> 01:00:38.120
of the modes of their
reported distribution.

01:00:38.120 --> 01:00:40.150
In particular, whichever mode is closest

01:00:40.150 --> 01:00:42.693
to their prior expected mean.

01:00:43.890 --> 01:00:45.230
So what this means is that

01:00:45.230 --> 01:00:47.270
for bi-modal reported distributions

01:00:47.270 --> 01:00:50.830
which can occur when
you have extremity bias

01:00:50.830 --> 01:00:53.763
or when you have fair
and balanced reporting,

01:00:56.480 --> 01:00:57.950
you can come up with situations.

01:00:57.950 --> 01:01:02.350
You find situations where
agents that have priors

01:01:02.350 --> 01:01:06.833
on one side converge to
the mode closest to them.

01:01:08.520 --> 01:01:12.760
Whereas agents that have beliefs
on the other side converge

01:01:12.760 --> 01:01:14.783
to the node that's closest to them.

01:01:15.919 --> 01:01:16.870
And these are gonna generally be different

01:01:16.870 --> 01:01:18.290
from one another.

01:01:18.290 --> 01:01:20.600
And in fact, what you can
see is that over time,

01:01:20.600 --> 01:01:23.033
beliefs can actually
diverge from one another.

01:01:23.980 --> 01:01:28.980
Priors that are close to one
another can become more distant

01:01:29.090 --> 01:01:30.920
with more data.

01:01:30.920 --> 01:01:32.750
And the reason that happens is

01:01:32.750 --> 01:01:36.160
even though the priors are
initially close to one another,

01:01:36.160 --> 01:01:38.730
nonetheless one is closer to
one mode and the other closer

01:01:38.730 --> 01:01:41.600
to the other mode, and
they approach the modes

01:01:41.600 --> 01:01:42.953
of the report distribution.

01:01:43.930 --> 01:01:46.200
Here's a figure that shows that happening.

01:01:46.200 --> 01:01:48.370
You have some true
distribution of evidence.

01:01:48.370 --> 01:01:51.200
You have here, some
combination of extremity bias

01:01:51.200 --> 01:01:55.110
and a fair reporting that leads

01:01:55.110 --> 01:02:00.110
to some distorted reported
distribution of evidence.

01:02:01.840 --> 01:02:05.700
And what you find is in
these two different charts

01:02:05.700 --> 01:02:07.733
on the right, the two pink ones,

01:02:09.840 --> 01:02:14.190
agents that begin with
slightly different priors

01:02:14.190 --> 01:02:18.513
where you see that in both
cases, their priors are,

01:02:19.647 --> 01:02:20.480
you know, they're less certain

01:02:20.480 --> 01:02:21.890
than they end up being, the priors,

01:02:24.276 --> 01:02:25.963
the less opaque Gaussian.

01:02:27.750 --> 01:02:29.570
You see is that although
they start kind of close

01:02:29.570 --> 01:02:32.290
to one another, over time, they separate

01:02:32.290 --> 01:02:36.130
and moreover, their confidence
becomes much greater.

01:02:36.130 --> 01:02:40.300
I.e. their expected value
for the standard deviation

01:02:40.300 --> 01:02:45.300
of the events in the
world becomes smaller.

01:02:45.760 --> 01:02:48.360
And so neither agent ends up

01:02:48.360 --> 01:02:52.060
approximating the true
distribution of events very well.

01:02:52.060 --> 01:02:54.520
They both have smaller variants
in the true distribution

01:02:54.520 --> 01:02:56.470
of events, and they get the mean wrong.

01:02:58.710 --> 01:03:02.722
Okay, so this is the kind
of polarization, right?

01:03:02.722 --> 01:03:04.410
It's a polarization where agents

01:03:04.410 --> 01:03:07.590
with different priors presented

01:03:07.590 --> 01:03:12.590
with the same evidence end
up with different posteriors

01:03:12.850 --> 01:03:15.360
and what's driving this results is the way

01:03:15.360 --> 01:03:17.810
that we've modeled confirmation
bias, along with the way

01:03:17.810 --> 01:03:21.173
in which the evidence is presented.

01:03:23.103 --> 01:03:25.900
What happens is in cases
where there's little

01:03:25.900 --> 01:03:29.090
or no data that lies
between the two modes,

01:03:29.090 --> 01:03:30.560
agents don't get pulled

01:03:30.560 --> 01:03:32.920
towards the true central behavior, right?

01:03:32.920 --> 01:03:35.840
So they never end up approximating W

01:03:35.840 --> 01:03:37.320
because all they ever see

01:03:37.320 --> 01:03:40.450
or all they ever actually
update on is data

01:03:40.450 --> 01:03:43.533
that lies near one of the two modes.

01:03:44.450 --> 01:03:47.360
Now, I think, you know,

01:03:47.360 --> 01:03:48.770
it's not really the
point of the talk today.

01:03:48.770 --> 01:03:49.680
I just wanna flag though,

01:03:49.680 --> 01:03:53.230
one very interesting results
of this work is that hyperbole

01:03:53.230 --> 01:03:56.240
which I think is arguably
the only of these biases

01:03:56.240 --> 01:04:01.240
that we really consider
problematic, generally speaking,

01:04:02.060 --> 01:04:06.058
or at least incompatible with
good journalistic practice,

01:04:06.058 --> 01:04:08.820
actually doesn't
contribute to polarization,

01:04:08.820 --> 01:04:11.490
unless you also have
fairness and extremity bias

01:04:12.400 --> 01:04:14.670
and extremity bias, especially

01:04:14.670 --> 01:04:17.136
to a lesser extent now fairness

01:04:17.136 --> 01:04:20.500
do conform with journalistic norms.

01:04:20.500 --> 01:04:23.400
I think that it's not
considered problematic

01:04:23.400 --> 01:04:26.750
for journalists to report only on things

01:04:26.750 --> 01:04:27.960
that are considered interesting

01:04:27.960 --> 01:04:31.213
or novel or out of the ordinary.

01:04:33.010 --> 01:04:35.420
Okay, so again, in the interest of time,

01:04:35.420 --> 01:04:37.616
I'm gonna do this briefly,
but I'm gonna ask now,

01:04:37.616 --> 01:04:40.690
what did these models
taken together tell us

01:04:40.690 --> 01:04:43.233
about the real-world
phenomenon of polarization?

01:04:44.390 --> 01:04:45.513
Which model is right?

01:04:46.710 --> 01:04:51.190
Now, I think that there's a
attempting line of thought here.

01:04:51.190 --> 01:04:53.310
Just to think about this sort of situation

01:04:53.310 --> 01:04:54.880
in a way analogous to how you might think

01:04:54.880 --> 01:04:57.363
about a scientific theory.

01:04:58.200 --> 01:05:00.930
So the idea here is you
come up with some model,

01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:03.940
that model reproduces some
qualitative phenomenon.

01:05:03.940 --> 01:05:06.860
You might take that fact,
prima facie evidence

01:05:06.860 --> 01:05:10.790
that some feature of the
model actually describes how,

01:05:10.790 --> 01:05:15.010
or some aspect of how
say a social situation

01:05:15.010 --> 01:05:18.300
or social group functions, right?

01:05:18.300 --> 01:05:19.650
This is a move that's very similar

01:05:19.650 --> 01:05:21.630
to a standard inferential pattern

01:05:21.630 --> 01:05:26.010
often attributed to scientists
whereby if you have a theory

01:05:26.010 --> 01:05:27.250
that makes some prediction.

01:05:27.250 --> 01:05:29.130
Say about the outcomes of some experiment

01:05:29.130 --> 01:05:32.680
or the tendency of some
phenomenon to occur,

01:05:32.680 --> 01:05:35.023
and then you find that
that phenomenon occurs,

01:05:35.870 --> 01:05:38.760
you infer that the theory
must be true or possible true

01:05:38.760 --> 01:05:40.430
or you increase your credence

01:05:40.430 --> 01:05:43.763
that that particular theory is accurate.

01:05:45.637 --> 01:05:48.680
Now there's a well-known defeater
for this sort of reasoning

01:05:49.900 --> 01:05:51.810
to the situation in which you
have, or sometimes called,

01:05:51.810 --> 01:05:54.000
under discrimination
of theory by evidence.

01:05:54.000 --> 01:05:58.720
That is you have many different theories

01:05:58.720 --> 01:06:01.620
that are apparently equally compatible

01:06:01.620 --> 01:06:02.993
with the observed data.

01:06:04.110 --> 01:06:06.580
So in a case like that, if
you have different theories

01:06:06.580 --> 01:06:08.780
that explain the data
and you don't have a way

01:06:08.780 --> 01:06:10.900
of choosing between them, you might say,

01:06:10.900 --> 01:06:14.687
well that undermines my
confidence in any of them.

01:06:14.687 --> 01:06:16.324
And so you might think

01:06:16.324 --> 01:06:19.190
that the right moral to draw from models

01:06:19.190 --> 01:06:21.150
of the sort that I've just
discussed is that, look,

01:06:21.150 --> 01:06:23.480
these models can't tell us anything

01:06:23.480 --> 01:06:27.230
about how real world polarization works.

01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:28.570
We can't infer anything from them.

01:06:28.570 --> 01:06:30.450
They disagree about the
underlying mechanism.

01:06:30.450 --> 01:06:32.619
They provide different explanations.

01:06:32.619 --> 01:06:37.619
And that means that we
can't choose between them.

01:06:37.710 --> 01:06:39.670
And if we can't choose between them,

01:06:39.670 --> 01:06:42.359
that means that we can't, for instance,

01:06:42.359 --> 01:06:45.740
use them to make any kinds
of policy recommendations.

01:06:45.740 --> 01:06:49.760
We can't use them to
think about, you know,

01:06:49.760 --> 01:06:52.340
how we got into a particular
epistemic situation

01:06:52.340 --> 01:06:54.100
and how we might get out of it.

01:06:54.100 --> 01:06:56.560
We can't use them to reason effectively

01:06:56.560 --> 01:06:58.363
about the world as we find it.

01:06:59.699 --> 01:07:02.740
And so you might think then
that really what we need

01:07:02.740 --> 01:07:05.650
to understand real world
polarization is something,

01:07:05.650 --> 01:07:06.880
something very different.

01:07:06.880 --> 01:07:08.790
So maybe some combination

01:07:08.790 --> 01:07:11.330
of fine-grained empirical evidence,

01:07:11.330 --> 01:07:14.890
something that can really
distinguish these cases

01:07:14.890 --> 01:07:15.723
from one another,

01:07:15.723 --> 01:07:17.630
help us understand what's really going on.

01:07:18.710 --> 01:07:21.150
Now, maybe there's still a
role for models of the sort

01:07:21.150 --> 01:07:26.150
or perhaps actually successor
models that are, you know,

01:07:26.850 --> 01:07:31.760
attempt to model the world
with greater detailed accuracy

01:07:33.050 --> 01:07:33.883
because then you might think,

01:07:33.883 --> 01:07:35.380
well what the models can do is come up

01:07:35.380 --> 01:07:39.410
with more specific and
discriminating predictions

01:07:39.410 --> 01:07:44.010
that we could then use to distinguish

01:07:44.010 --> 01:07:45.470
between the possible explanations

01:07:45.470 --> 01:07:48.943
of polarization presented
by each of these models.

01:07:51.470 --> 01:07:52.510
Now, I wanna suggest

01:07:52.510 --> 01:07:55.430
that that is not at all
the right moral to draw

01:07:55.430 --> 01:07:56.530
from the situation.

01:07:56.530 --> 01:07:58.820
That in fact, we can draw much stronger

01:07:58.820 --> 01:08:03.820
and more important morals
from the models as presented.

01:08:04.870 --> 01:08:05.770
Why is that?

01:08:05.770 --> 01:08:10.050
Well, the kind of dialectic I
just presented seems to take

01:08:10.050 --> 01:08:12.710
for granted that if these models disagree

01:08:12.710 --> 01:08:15.430
about the underlying
cause of polarization,

01:08:15.430 --> 01:08:17.940
then they are incompatible, right?

01:08:17.940 --> 01:08:20.070
And this is the sort of thing
you might think to be true

01:08:20.070 --> 01:08:21.660
if you're talking about,

01:08:21.660 --> 01:08:26.660
say theories in physics where
different theories, you know,

01:08:27.261 --> 01:08:28.660
if you have two different
theories of the electron,

01:08:28.660 --> 01:08:30.990
you might think only one
of them can be right.

01:08:30.990 --> 01:08:33.520
And so if both of them
reproduce the data you have,

01:08:33.520 --> 01:08:35.110
you don't have a way of
choosing between them,

01:08:35.110 --> 01:08:38.380
even though you know only
one of them could be correct,

01:08:38.380 --> 01:08:40.390
but that's not what's going on here.

01:08:40.390 --> 01:08:41.620
It's not the case

01:08:41.620 --> 01:08:44.659
that these different
mechanisms are incompatible.

01:08:44.659 --> 01:08:49.050
Rather, I think what we see here is

01:08:49.050 --> 01:08:52.710
that there are apparently
multiple sufficient causes

01:08:52.710 --> 01:08:55.601
for the social phenomenon of polarization.

01:08:55.601 --> 01:08:56.830
Now, as I said at the beginning,

01:08:56.830 --> 01:08:58.750
I don't at all mean to suggest

01:08:58.750 --> 01:09:01.670
that what I've given here
is an exhaustive list

01:09:01.670 --> 01:09:03.830
or even the most important examples

01:09:03.830 --> 01:09:07.730
of possible causes of polarization.

01:09:07.730 --> 01:09:10.920
I just claim that these
are models that suggest

01:09:10.920 --> 01:09:12.440
that a certain kind

01:09:12.440 --> 01:09:17.440
of mechanism may be
sufficient for polarization,

01:09:19.263 --> 01:09:23.500
but all of these mechanisms
can be present together, right?

01:09:23.500 --> 01:09:25.210
It can be that we have confirmation bias.

01:09:25.210 --> 01:09:27.127
In fact, it appears we
do have confirmation bias

01:09:27.127 --> 01:09:29.177
and it may be that we
have conformity bias.

01:09:29.177 --> 01:09:32.179
And in fact, it appears we
do have conformity bias.

01:09:32.179 --> 01:09:34.240
And it may be that we
tend to trust some sources

01:09:34.240 --> 01:09:35.600
of evidence more than others,

01:09:35.600 --> 01:09:40.180
perhaps we condition based
on the epistemic success

01:09:40.180 --> 01:09:43.780
of the originator of the evidence,

01:09:43.780 --> 01:09:46.533
which apparently is
something that we do do.

01:09:47.980 --> 01:09:50.700
So all of these can be
operative at the same time.

01:09:50.700 --> 01:09:51.910
Now, what that means is that

01:09:51.910 --> 01:09:54.990
even if you had more
fine-grained empirical data

01:09:56.100 --> 01:09:59.510
and predictions that tried to distinguish

01:09:59.510 --> 01:10:01.640
between the different models,

01:10:01.640 --> 01:10:04.780
the fact that you could get good evidence

01:10:04.780 --> 01:10:07.020
that one of these mechanisms is present

01:10:07.020 --> 01:10:11.130
and is leading to
polarization, does not imply

01:10:11.130 --> 01:10:13.950
that the other ones are not also present

01:10:13.950 --> 01:10:16.263
and also contributing to polarization.

01:10:17.340 --> 01:10:19.210
So, in fact, I think that the right way

01:10:19.210 --> 01:10:21.823
of thinking about what's going on here is

01:10:21.823 --> 01:10:26.720
that we see that evidence for
a particular hypothesis here

01:10:26.720 --> 01:10:28.310
about what causes polarization

01:10:29.170 --> 01:10:32.980
isn't evidence against other alternative.

01:10:32.980 --> 01:10:34.360
You might've thought competing,

01:10:34.360 --> 01:10:38.033
but in fact complimentary
explanations of polarization.

01:10:39.090 --> 01:10:44.090
And so I take that to be the basis

01:10:44.180 --> 01:10:46.290
for a strong cautionary note,

01:10:46.290 --> 01:10:50.030
but how to think about
policy making intended

01:10:50.030 --> 01:10:54.674
to somehow ameliorate polarization,

01:10:54.674 --> 01:10:57.803
particularly say polarization
and social media.

01:10:59.137 --> 01:11:02.380
The idea is that policy
interventions tend to focus

01:11:02.380 --> 01:11:04.990
on unitary explanations of the phenomenon.

01:11:04.990 --> 01:11:06.840
There are some positive cause,

01:11:06.840 --> 01:11:09.230
some idea that if you
intervene on that cause

01:11:09.230 --> 01:11:12.060
through policymaking, say changing the way

01:11:12.060 --> 01:11:13.890
that algorithms can work on Facebook,

01:11:13.890 --> 01:11:17.540
changing the way that Twitter works,

01:11:17.540 --> 01:11:20.290
number of Twitter followers
someone can have, whatever.

01:11:21.530 --> 01:11:23.990
That by intervening on
that particular variable,

01:11:23.990 --> 01:11:28.990
you're going to have some
clear impact, influence

01:11:31.680 --> 01:11:35.880
on the thing that you're trying to change,

01:11:35.880 --> 01:11:37.180
in this case polarization.

01:11:38.760 --> 01:11:39.870
But if it's true

01:11:39.870 --> 01:11:42.230
that there are multiple
mutually compatible,

01:11:42.230 --> 01:11:45.430
independently sufficient
possible causes for polarization

01:11:46.460 --> 01:11:47.293
and it's not clear

01:11:47.293 --> 01:11:49.281
that any single explanation
is going to suffice.

01:11:49.281 --> 01:11:51.770
And that means that policy interventions

01:11:51.770 --> 01:11:56.770
that target a single cause are
not only unlikely to succeed

01:11:56.770 --> 01:11:59.440
on their own, but may even backfire

01:11:59.440 --> 01:12:01.410
because what you need to be attentive

01:12:01.410 --> 01:12:02.663
to is the way in which,

01:12:03.830 --> 01:12:08.220
by trying to block one mechanism,
which is actually present,

01:12:08.220 --> 01:12:11.310
you can unwittingly
amplify another mechanism

01:12:11.310 --> 01:12:13.440
which is also present.

01:12:13.440 --> 01:12:15.700
Just as an example, suppose
you're the sort of person

01:12:15.700 --> 01:12:20.110
that thinks that conformity
and just you know,

01:12:20.110 --> 01:12:23.641
poor connections between
different networks of people.

01:12:23.641 --> 01:12:25.841
This is, Priya mentioned,
the filter bubble idea,

01:12:25.841 --> 01:12:26.674
but that's basically what's going on

01:12:27.936 --> 01:12:28.769
on this sort of proposal.

01:12:28.769 --> 01:12:30.810
The idea would be well, what's
going on is that you have,

01:12:30.810 --> 01:12:33.200
you know, people
sometimes say red internet

01:12:33.200 --> 01:12:34.033
and a blue internet.

01:12:34.033 --> 01:12:36.470
Republican's see some
things when they go online.

01:12:36.470 --> 01:12:38.480
Democrats see other things
when they go online.

01:12:38.480 --> 01:12:40.600
They're updating on different stuff.

01:12:40.600 --> 01:12:42.900
They're conforming with different groups

01:12:42.900 --> 01:12:45.760
and it's by sort of separating those

01:12:45.760 --> 01:12:47.097
that you end up with polarization.

01:12:47.097 --> 01:12:50.930
And so if we increased
the connections, right.

01:12:50.930 --> 01:12:54.530
Showed more Democrats,
more Republican media

01:12:54.530 --> 01:12:56.960
and showed more Republicans,
more Democratic media,

01:12:56.960 --> 01:12:58.410
you might think what's gonna happen is

01:12:58.410 --> 01:13:00.160
you're gonna have some convergence.

01:13:01.100 --> 01:13:03.230
Well, it couldn't be that conformity

01:13:03.230 --> 01:13:05.970
and these network effects
are playing a role,

01:13:05.970 --> 01:13:09.220
but nonetheless, trust dynamics
is also playing a role.

01:13:09.220 --> 01:13:11.340
In which case, by exposing more,

01:13:11.340 --> 01:13:14.100
exposing people more to the other side,

01:13:14.100 --> 01:13:16.480
if they distrust the other side enough

01:13:16.480 --> 01:13:18.030
that they're not gonna make any difference

01:13:18.030 --> 01:13:19.660
or may under some circumstances

01:13:19.660 --> 01:13:23.853
even make people tend to polarize more.

01:13:24.793 --> 01:13:29.793
For instance using something like, well,

01:13:29.977 --> 01:13:33.160
under some modifications
of the trust dynamics can

01:13:33.160 --> 01:13:35.583
actually lead them to polarize more.

01:13:36.450 --> 01:13:40.070
And so what this means
practically speaking is

01:13:40.070 --> 01:13:44.250
that empirical work
cannot be directed only

01:13:44.250 --> 01:13:47.820
to positively identifying a
specific causal factors, right?

01:13:47.820 --> 01:13:52.080
Not only to showing that
some factor is operative

01:13:52.080 --> 01:13:55.080
in some contexts, but also

01:13:55.080 --> 01:13:59.680
to establishing what other
factors are also operative

01:14:00.640 --> 01:14:04.730
and to weighing the
relative causal contribution

01:14:04.730 --> 01:14:07.970
of the possibly many
different causal factors.

01:14:07.970 --> 01:14:10.840
This is a much more
difficult kind of activity

01:14:10.840 --> 01:14:14.650
and something that I
think it's very, you know,

01:14:14.650 --> 01:14:17.210
we don't have great
methods to my knowledge

01:14:17.210 --> 01:14:19.210
of establishing this

01:14:19.210 --> 01:14:23.630
kind of relative amount of significance.

01:14:23.630 --> 01:14:25.500
But it seems to me that
in a situation like this

01:14:25.500 --> 01:14:28.940
where we have multiple sufficient causes

01:14:28.940 --> 01:14:33.340
for a social phenomenon
and some reason to think

01:14:33.340 --> 01:14:37.100
that all of them may be
present and may be important,

01:14:37.100 --> 01:14:39.820
this is what we need
to figure out how to do

01:14:39.820 --> 01:14:42.190
in order to be able to properly evaluate

01:14:42.190 --> 01:14:44.010
any kind of policy proposal

01:14:44.010 --> 01:14:46.770
that is gonna be successfully sensitive

01:14:46.770 --> 01:14:49.913
to the full complexity of the situation.

01:14:51.190 --> 01:14:52.023
Thank you.

01:14:55.410 --> 01:14:57.040
- Thank you so much, James.

01:14:57.040 --> 01:14:59.293
That was absolutely fantastic.

01:14:59.293 --> 01:15:03.050
And so I would like to invite everyone

01:15:03.050 --> 01:15:07.380
to unmute and clap, yes.

01:15:07.380 --> 01:15:08.860
Much to think about.

01:15:08.860 --> 01:15:13.750
So if I may, before opening
up the floor to questions,

01:15:13.750 --> 01:15:15.403
ask you one myself.

01:15:17.170 --> 01:15:20.750
I was just, I mean, I really
like your caution showing

01:15:20.750 --> 01:15:22.520
that there are many
ways to think about it,

01:15:22.520 --> 01:15:24.970
but that we need to really be cautious

01:15:24.970 --> 01:15:28.540
about concluding that
there's one simple solution

01:15:28.540 --> 01:15:30.940
or that we have captured
the under, you know,

01:15:30.940 --> 01:15:35.540
the actual real world
polarization adequately

01:15:35.540 --> 01:15:37.810
with any of these models

01:15:37.810 --> 01:15:40.580
to really chase off the
one solution or the other.

01:15:40.580 --> 01:15:43.850
I think I wanted to
come back to this issue

01:15:43.850 --> 01:15:46.080
that you did raise in the beginning

01:15:46.080 --> 01:15:49.120
and bring in the work of Dan Kahan

01:15:49.120 --> 01:15:53.270
who is at the Center for
Cultural Cognition at Yale.

01:15:53.270 --> 01:15:55.197
Well, you know, that he's
been studying as, you know,

01:15:55.197 --> 01:15:57.070
the sort of disbelief

01:15:57.070 --> 01:15:59.330
in climate change and science denialism.

01:15:59.330 --> 01:16:04.330
So it sounds like, you know,
this landscape of priors

01:16:04.370 --> 01:16:07.050
that people have that, you know,

01:16:07.050 --> 01:16:12.050
these are informed by very,
by our tribal identities

01:16:12.870 --> 01:16:17.870
and they are part and parcel
of a larger worldview.

01:16:18.800 --> 01:16:22.000
So one of the things he,
his findings often show

01:16:22.000 --> 01:16:25.770
that there are sort of
these two broadish mindsets,

01:16:25.770 --> 01:16:27.890
sort of a more collectivist mindset

01:16:27.890 --> 01:16:30.520
and a more individualist mindset.

01:16:30.520 --> 01:16:35.520
And that this mindset is
independent of one's position

01:16:35.560 --> 01:16:40.010
on any one particular
issue that is a broad lens

01:16:40.010 --> 01:16:42.130
with which people see the whole world.

01:16:42.130 --> 01:16:44.710
And therefore then every
issue gets refracted

01:16:44.710 --> 01:16:45.910
through that lens.

01:16:45.910 --> 01:16:49.010
So I was very curious, you know,

01:16:49.010 --> 01:16:53.260
in these modeling approaches,
you obviously had room

01:16:53.260 --> 01:16:58.260
for priors and stuff, but
I was curious, what can,

01:16:58.440 --> 01:17:01.460
if anything be done about what seems

01:17:01.460 --> 01:17:06.460
like a fundamental,
psychological, sociological schism

01:17:07.700 --> 01:17:10.230
between personality
types that is, you know,

01:17:10.230 --> 01:17:13.163
very historically, culturally,
politically rooted.

01:17:14.821 --> 01:17:15.883
And you know, the same sort

01:17:15.883 --> 01:17:16.730
of simplistic arguments
have been about, you know,

01:17:16.730 --> 01:17:19.900
east versus west, collective
versus individual, et cetera.

01:17:19.900 --> 01:17:24.180
So I was very curious how
you would see that kind of,

01:17:24.180 --> 01:17:29.180
you know, broader view of the
world, institutions, people,

01:17:29.840 --> 01:17:34.530
et cetera, being folded into thinking

01:17:34.530 --> 01:17:37.433
about how it sort of
manifests in polarization.

01:17:39.458 --> 01:17:44.458
- So I think I have
maybe two things to say.

01:17:48.680 --> 01:17:53.680
One is that I see it as a kind
of yes and sort of situation

01:17:58.220 --> 01:18:02.327
where I think, you know,

01:18:04.540 --> 01:18:06.560
each of the kinds of mechanisms
that I pointed to you,

01:18:06.560 --> 01:18:10.260
there's psychological
evidence that we engage

01:18:10.260 --> 01:18:11.890
in this sort of behavior,

01:18:11.890 --> 01:18:14.500
that we do differentially
trust evidential sources,

01:18:14.500 --> 01:18:16.233
that we do prefer to conform.

01:18:18.710 --> 01:18:22.140
There's psychological
evidence to support this idea

01:18:22.140 --> 01:18:27.140
of background either ideologies

01:18:27.500 --> 01:18:29.540
or perhaps even something more basic

01:18:29.540 --> 01:18:31.570
about how we think about the world, right?

01:18:31.570 --> 01:18:32.810
I mean, I think sometimes

01:18:32.810 --> 01:18:34.410
in this literature, it's not totally clear

01:18:34.410 --> 01:18:36.787
that you can distinguish a case

01:18:36.787 --> 01:18:37.860
where it's culturally constructed

01:18:37.860 --> 01:18:39.770
and ideological and people
are sort of subscribed

01:18:39.770 --> 01:18:43.040
to a set of beliefs and
the case where people

01:18:43.040 --> 01:18:46.190
actually self sort based on the strength

01:18:46.190 --> 01:18:47.830
of their discussed reaction.

01:18:47.830 --> 01:18:50.428
I mean, so you see papers like
this saying, well you know,

01:18:50.428 --> 01:18:53.410
conservatives tend to have a
stronger discussed reaction

01:18:53.410 --> 01:18:56.100
or tend to have different
kinds of reactions

01:18:56.100 --> 01:18:58.403
to fear or to outsiders.

01:18:59.640 --> 01:19:01.266
It's sort of, it's hard to know

01:19:01.266 --> 01:19:04.810
I think at this stage,
other people may know more,

01:19:04.810 --> 01:19:07.580
but it's hard for me to know at this stage

01:19:07.580 --> 01:19:08.990
whether we should think of that

01:19:10.607 --> 01:19:12.240
as cultural or innate across individuals.

01:19:12.240 --> 01:19:13.970
But once again, it's something that

01:19:13.970 --> 01:19:15.700
even if that weren't there,

01:19:15.700 --> 01:19:18.130
other aspects of human
psychology seem to be sufficient

01:19:18.130 --> 01:19:20.033
to explain polarization, right?

01:19:21.000 --> 01:19:25.170
And so if we focus all of our attention

01:19:25.170 --> 01:19:30.170
on trying to sort of solve
this underlying tribalistic

01:19:30.506 --> 01:19:35.020
sort of tendency, I think that we're going

01:19:35.020 --> 01:19:38.830
to miss other things that
are probably also mattering.

01:19:38.830 --> 01:19:39.663
That's one thing.

01:19:39.663 --> 01:19:43.730
The second thing is that I have,

01:19:43.730 --> 01:19:45.421
Cailin and I have another paper together

01:19:45.421 --> 01:19:50.421
where we push back against
some of these ideas

01:19:50.487 --> 01:19:55.487
that the right way of explaining
political tribalism is

01:19:55.670 --> 01:19:59.480
via some organizing
principle like ideology

01:19:59.480 --> 01:20:01.740
which isn't exactly the same
as what you're describing,

01:20:01.740 --> 01:20:02.990
but it's closely related.

01:20:04.440 --> 01:20:07.030
And so George Lakoff, for instance,

01:20:07.030 --> 01:20:08.070
at Berkeley has this idea

01:20:08.070 --> 01:20:09.580
that there are two basic ideologies,

01:20:09.580 --> 01:20:12.160
the nurturing mother and the strict father

01:20:12.160 --> 01:20:14.070
and everyone falls into one or the other.

01:20:14.070 --> 01:20:15.440
So when you disagree about gun control

01:20:15.440 --> 01:20:18.400
or you disagree about, you know, COVID,

01:20:18.400 --> 01:20:19.980
you aren't disagreeing about those things,

01:20:19.980 --> 01:20:21.950
you're disagreeing about
whether you, you know,

01:20:21.950 --> 01:20:24.850
the state should be a nurturing
mother or a strict father.

01:20:25.950 --> 01:20:29.440
And we suggest that
there are some examples

01:20:29.440 --> 01:20:33.470
where there appear to be
correlations between values

01:20:33.470 --> 01:20:37.300
or beliefs that aren't
really clearly explained

01:20:37.300 --> 01:20:39.440
by this kind of ideological explanation

01:20:39.440 --> 01:20:42.030
unless the ideology becomes so vague

01:20:42.030 --> 01:20:45.410
that really anything can be
associated with either of them.

01:20:45.410 --> 01:20:48.160
- Right, but I guess, but also
you know, there's a tendency,

01:20:48.160 --> 01:20:50.630
right, especially in Western epistemology

01:20:50.630 --> 01:20:54.100
to be very reductive and
have like dichotomies,

01:20:54.100 --> 01:20:56.010
always break things into dichotomies.

01:20:56.010 --> 01:20:58.350
So it's like, you know,
it's either this or that.

01:20:58.350 --> 01:21:01.830
And there's like nothing sort
of interrelating in between.

01:21:01.830 --> 01:21:04.400
So there's that problem as
well with that position,

01:21:04.400 --> 01:21:08.370
as well as what you are suggesting is a,

01:21:08.370 --> 01:21:09.950
so there's a deeper problem

01:21:09.950 --> 01:21:13.800
with those kinds of positions
that are very reductionist

01:21:13.800 --> 01:21:16.190
in some way, right,
reducing everything to--

01:21:16.190 --> 01:21:18.990
- So what I would say though,
is I mean, so what we do

01:21:18.990 --> 01:21:22.810
in this paper is we show
that actually you can arrive

01:21:22.810 --> 01:21:24.560
at similar kinds of correlations

01:21:24.560 --> 01:21:28.740
between beliefs with a
kind of trust dynamics

01:21:28.740 --> 01:21:30.490
where there's no organizing principle,

01:21:30.490 --> 01:21:32.460
it's entirely indogenous.

01:21:32.460 --> 01:21:34.780
You get epistemic factions
where you have a whole bunch

01:21:34.780 --> 01:21:36.750
of different beliefs,
totally unrelated beliefs

01:21:36.750 --> 01:21:38.630
but where agents sort of clump.

01:21:38.630 --> 01:21:40.920
You know, you've got all
the ones who believe action,

01:21:40.920 --> 01:21:42.850
you know, who prefer actions A, A prime,

01:21:42.850 --> 01:21:44.990
A double prime, A triple prime,

01:21:44.990 --> 01:21:45.823
even though they're totally unrelated.

01:21:45.823 --> 01:21:46.860
And then the ones that prefer B,

01:21:46.860 --> 01:21:49.320
and B prime and B double prime and so on,

01:21:49.320 --> 01:21:51.520
without needing to introduce any kind

01:21:51.520 --> 01:21:55.390
of organizational principle
or background ideology

01:21:55.390 --> 01:21:59.830
or anything to sort of, it
just happens via a process

01:21:59.830 --> 01:22:02.780
where you know, I agree
with you on gun control,

01:22:02.780 --> 01:22:04.630
so I'm gonna trust your
views about climate change.

01:22:04.630 --> 01:22:05.540
And I agree with you about that,

01:22:05.540 --> 01:22:07.260
so I'm gonna trust your views about COVID.

01:22:07.260 --> 01:22:10.050
And one example that we point to is,

01:22:10.050 --> 01:22:11.890
and this is controversial,

01:22:11.890 --> 01:22:14.840
but I really liked the
example is, you know,

01:22:14.840 --> 01:22:17.270
last March hydroxychloroquine became

01:22:17.270 --> 01:22:19.370
the conservative treatment for COVID

01:22:19.370 --> 01:22:24.370
and Remdesivir became the
liberal treatment for COVID

01:22:25.940 --> 01:22:28.530
at a a point where there was,

01:22:28.530 --> 01:22:31.640
far before there was clear evidence on it.

01:22:31.640 --> 01:22:35.400
So you sort of, you see
this kind of people sorting

01:22:35.400 --> 01:22:38.180
into beliefs about matters
of fact that appear

01:22:38.180 --> 01:22:41.550
to depend on their background, you know,

01:22:41.550 --> 01:22:43.670
other background views, but in a way,

01:22:43.670 --> 01:22:45.250
I don't see any meaningful sense

01:22:45.250 --> 01:22:48.850
in which hydroxychloroquine
is the strict father solution

01:22:48.850 --> 01:22:52.630
to COVID and Remdesivir is
the nurturing mother solution

01:22:52.630 --> 01:22:57.010
to COVID or the collective
versus anti collectivist one.

01:22:57.010 --> 01:22:59.050
It seems like this is just
something random happened,

01:22:59.050 --> 01:22:59.883
but they sorted.

01:23:03.750 --> 01:23:08.330
- So we have, let me see,
you can hear me, right.

01:23:08.330 --> 01:23:12.870
Okay, so we have a question
from Sanjeev Kumar on the chat.

01:23:12.870 --> 01:23:16.260
Do you think polarization per
se may not be problematic,

01:23:16.260 --> 01:23:19.290
but the fact that we have
not developed a framework

01:23:19.290 --> 01:23:22.170
that can rationalize a pursuit

01:23:22.170 --> 01:23:24.520
for an underlying unity of things.

01:23:24.520 --> 01:23:27.323
We don't say that our legs are polarized.

01:23:29.120 --> 01:23:34.120
- So I think there are two
separate sorts of issues here.

01:23:35.670 --> 01:23:38.670
One is whether or not polarization
is by itself problematic.

01:23:39.825 --> 01:23:42.281
And I think there are
good reasons to think

01:23:42.281 --> 01:23:44.853
that polarization is not
by itself problematic,

01:23:46.950 --> 01:23:51.060
especially if polarization
is in some way transient.

01:23:51.060 --> 01:23:53.400
I think that there are
very good reasons to think

01:23:53.400 --> 01:23:57.017
that one reason why you
might have mechanisms

01:23:58.840 --> 01:24:00.590
that slow down learning

01:24:01.664 --> 01:24:05.670
and maybe preserve
transient polarization is

01:24:05.670 --> 01:24:09.790
because it can take a
long time to get reliable,

01:24:09.790 --> 01:24:13.060
consistent, representative
data about the world.

01:24:13.060 --> 01:24:16.370
And, you know, a situation where you have,

01:24:16.370 --> 01:24:18.310
for instance different cultural groups

01:24:18.310 --> 01:24:19.530
or even different individuals

01:24:19.530 --> 01:24:22.560
performing different
practices and learning

01:24:22.560 --> 01:24:24.530
from those practices is one perhaps

01:24:24.530 --> 01:24:25.980
where you can expect to achieve

01:24:25.980 --> 01:24:27.973
better overall outcomes over time.

01:24:29.880 --> 01:24:32.130
And so you might think
that some of the mechanisms

01:24:32.130 --> 01:24:34.650
that lead to polarization
are actually mechanisms

01:24:34.650 --> 01:24:37.720
that over the very long
run benefit social learning

01:24:37.720 --> 01:24:40.257
or benefit cultural evolution
or something like that

01:24:40.257 --> 01:24:42.637
and may even have been, you know,

01:24:44.480 --> 01:24:46.133
in some sense selected for.

01:24:48.800 --> 01:24:50.050
So I don't think polarization

01:24:50.050 --> 01:24:53.310
by itself is necessarily problematic.

01:24:53.310 --> 01:24:57.680
The idea though, that
some of these, you know,

01:24:57.680 --> 01:25:01.821
really quite dichotomous
situations, these propositions

01:25:01.821 --> 01:25:05.204
where there's a lot of evidence.

01:25:05.204 --> 01:25:09.170
And yet nonetheless, there
seems to be a very long

01:25:10.220 --> 01:25:13.803
and apparently quite
damaging disagreement.

01:25:14.778 --> 01:25:18.510
I don't wanna think, I don't tend to think

01:25:18.510 --> 01:25:19.750
that the right way of thinking

01:25:19.750 --> 01:25:24.410
about such situations is
one where there really are

01:25:24.410 --> 01:25:25.740
just two ways of being

01:25:25.740 --> 01:25:27.600
in the world and they're equally right.

01:25:27.600 --> 01:25:29.720
Or, you know, in a way suggested

01:25:29.720 --> 01:25:34.720
by this idea that our
legs are not polarized.

01:25:35.610 --> 01:25:37.324
Our legs are chiral.

01:25:37.324 --> 01:25:40.313
I mean, I guess that's a
different, physics joke.

01:25:43.080 --> 01:25:44.610
- Any other questions,

01:25:44.610 --> 01:25:47.123
please feel free to
type them into the chat.

01:25:51.270 --> 01:25:53.250
Wait, I have another question.

01:25:53.250 --> 01:25:57.230
So I will again, take the
liberty James, to ask you.

01:25:57.230 --> 01:26:02.230
So I wondered whether you found

01:26:03.350 --> 01:26:07.970
that for particular issues,

01:26:07.970 --> 01:26:12.340
some models were better descriptors.

01:26:12.340 --> 01:26:14.330
I'm not talking about the
solutions, because I mean,

01:26:14.330 --> 01:26:16.880
I think I totally take
your point that, you know,

01:26:16.880 --> 01:26:19.620
it would be just too simplistic
to just say that, okay,

01:26:19.620 --> 01:26:20.970
this is the model,

01:26:20.970 --> 01:26:22.770
and therefore these are all the solutions

01:26:22.770 --> 01:26:26.010
that can describe how to
mitigate the polarization.

01:26:26.010 --> 01:26:27.070
But I was curious.

01:26:27.070 --> 01:26:32.070
So for example, if you took
sort of the polarization

01:26:32.510 --> 01:26:35.133
on the issue of vaccination, for example,

01:26:37.010 --> 01:26:39.653
versus climate change.

01:26:40.970 --> 01:26:45.160
Did you find in the
modeling approach that,

01:26:45.160 --> 01:26:50.160
you know, one set of models
was like a better descriptor

01:26:50.350 --> 01:26:51.183
of the problem?

01:26:51.183 --> 01:26:52.710
Because I think that's the
point that you're trying

01:26:52.710 --> 01:26:56.620
to make that it's very hard to figure out

01:26:56.620 --> 01:27:00.240
and restrict ourselves
to one unique model,

01:27:00.240 --> 01:27:03.660
but I wondered if there
were particular kinds.

01:27:03.660 --> 01:27:05.250
You know, I'm just trying
to see if there are ways

01:27:05.250 --> 01:27:07.310
to map particular issues

01:27:07.310 --> 01:27:09.903
to particular kinds of agent models.

01:27:12.271 --> 01:27:15.030
And whether you found that,
whether there's something there

01:27:15.030 --> 01:27:18.240
that you found interesting for, you know,

01:27:18.240 --> 01:27:23.030
for matters that have to do
with, you know, personal being,

01:27:23.030 --> 01:27:25.220
you know, like a medical
issue, which has to do

01:27:25.220 --> 01:27:29.440
with our personhood, much
more immediate salience

01:27:29.440 --> 01:27:32.872
than climate change, which
for many people feels

01:27:32.872 --> 01:27:35.720
like it's quite distanced
because they are not accepting

01:27:35.720 --> 01:27:37.790
that it has touched us
in our lives already.

01:27:37.790 --> 01:27:41.340
So I was just curious
about like the proximity

01:27:41.340 --> 01:27:43.960
and you know, what are the
other kinds of beliefs?

01:27:47.913 --> 01:27:51.520
What are the beliefs can
be classified in some way

01:27:51.520 --> 01:27:55.083
in terms of particular model
being a better descriptor?

01:27:58.539 --> 01:28:00.540
- So to an extent, I mean,

01:28:00.540 --> 01:28:05.283
I wanna emphasize again, that
the models here are so simple

01:28:09.490 --> 01:28:13.880
that I'm reluctant to confidently assert

01:28:13.880 --> 01:28:16.630
that what's going on in
any of these models is

01:28:16.630 --> 01:28:19.163
what's going on in any
particular real-world case.

01:28:20.857 --> 01:28:24.620
That said, I do think

01:28:24.620 --> 01:28:29.620
that some of the qualitative
features of the models do seem

01:28:30.180 --> 01:28:33.283
to invoke particular
cases particularly well.

01:28:34.410 --> 01:28:37.640
And so for instance, one
of the things that happens

01:28:37.640 --> 01:28:40.610
in the conformity model is
that you have a trade off

01:28:40.610 --> 01:28:43.060
between how much you care
about successful action

01:28:43.060 --> 01:28:45.010
and how much you care about conformity.

01:28:45.890 --> 01:28:48.310
Now, normally when we present
the model, we think about it

01:28:48.310 --> 01:28:51.143
in terms of the strength of
your preference to conform.

01:28:52.352 --> 01:28:55.120
But another sort of dual way

01:28:55.120 --> 01:28:58.530
of thinking about it is the weakness

01:28:58.530 --> 01:29:01.573
of the benefits of performing
the successful action.

01:29:02.680 --> 01:29:07.170
And so there are some
sorts of cases where,

01:29:07.170 --> 01:29:09.030
although there is a matter of fact

01:29:09.030 --> 01:29:11.860
and there is a lot of evidence available,

01:29:11.860 --> 01:29:13.830
nonetheless as an individual,

01:29:13.830 --> 01:29:18.180
the decisions you make aren't going

01:29:18.180 --> 01:29:19.940
to have particularly
strong effects one way

01:29:19.940 --> 01:29:23.410
or the other, even you
know, there's polarization

01:29:23.410 --> 01:29:26.593
in the US regarding evolutionary theory.

01:29:27.910 --> 01:29:29.410
Again, it's a case where
there's an enormous amount

01:29:29.410 --> 01:29:30.640
of evidence available,

01:29:30.640 --> 01:29:33.523
but there's absolutely
nothing that hurt like,

01:29:33.523 --> 01:29:36.660
there's no way in which
you're individually hurt

01:29:36.660 --> 01:29:39.830
going around and doing
things as an individual

01:29:39.830 --> 01:29:42.090
that are incompatible
with evolutionary theory,

01:29:42.090 --> 01:29:43.470
which for most people just mean saying,

01:29:43.470 --> 01:29:45.300
I don't believe in
evolutionary theory, right?

01:29:45.300 --> 01:29:46.570
That doesn't harm you in some way,

01:29:46.570 --> 01:29:47.900
but you do get the benefits perhaps,

01:29:47.900 --> 01:29:50.470
in conforming within your community.

01:29:50.470 --> 01:29:52.700
Climate change is a bit like
that because the consequences

01:29:52.700 --> 01:29:55.693
of your actions tend to be
remote in space and time.

01:29:57.520 --> 01:30:00.548
But other things like, you know,

01:30:00.548 --> 01:30:05.548
it's harder to see some of
the behaviors we've seen

01:30:07.850 --> 01:30:12.850
around COVID as an example
of that kind of behavior.

01:30:15.820 --> 01:30:17.480
On the other hand, I mean,

01:30:17.480 --> 01:30:20.253
Cailin and I actually
considered, we didn't do it.

01:30:20.253 --> 01:30:22.303
We considered writing an op-ed describing

01:30:29.220 --> 01:30:32.190
how COVID perhaps reflected
just a complete failure

01:30:32.190 --> 01:30:37.107
of classical decision theory,
where you think that you can,

01:30:39.849 --> 01:30:43.003
you know, that people are
motivated by immediate benefits

01:30:44.530 --> 01:30:47.670
or harms, and so sort
of directly seeing this

01:30:47.670 --> 01:30:49.720
kind of behavior led to
these kinds of outcomes

01:30:49.720 --> 01:30:51.480
for these kinds of people
who are close to me.

01:30:51.480 --> 01:30:53.830
I see a directly that
would affect your behavior.

01:30:53.830 --> 01:30:55.780
But that, in fact, it
seems like other sorts

01:30:55.780 --> 01:30:58.590
of considerations could counterbalance

01:30:58.590 --> 01:31:01.700
that kind of direct,
immediate evidence, right.

01:31:01.700 --> 01:31:02.800
Especially now, right?

01:31:02.800 --> 01:31:05.850
I mean maybe last March
or April or May, you know,

01:31:05.850 --> 01:31:10.200
you're not in New York,
you're not in Paris.

01:31:10.200 --> 01:31:15.143
You know, maybe you don't trust
what's your hearing about,

01:31:15.977 --> 01:31:20.977
but by now, I think people have
much more direct experience

01:31:22.180 --> 01:31:24.423
and yet many people still are,

01:31:25.700 --> 01:31:27.350
act as if they're very skeptical.

01:31:28.910 --> 01:31:32.290
- So we have a question from Jenny Wagner.

01:31:32.290 --> 01:31:34.330
Is the timing of the update

01:31:34.330 --> 01:31:38.250
of information for each
agent a critical issue.

01:31:38.250 --> 01:31:41.770
Imagine you update information
of the agents asynchronously.

01:31:41.770 --> 01:31:45.110
Would that change the behavior
of the entire network?

01:31:45.110 --> 01:31:47.880
- So there are a few different
kinds of effects here.

01:31:47.880 --> 01:31:52.500
One has to do with how often
agents perform their action

01:31:52.500 --> 01:31:53.850
before sharing evidence, right?

01:31:53.850 --> 01:31:55.642
So you can think about this

01:31:55.642 --> 01:31:57.230
as how large a sample do you collect

01:31:57.230 --> 01:32:00.540
before stopping and your beliefs, right?

01:32:00.540 --> 01:32:04.200
Because when that's large,
you know, you're gonna go

01:32:04.200 --> 01:32:06.010
with what you originally
think is the better action.

01:32:06.010 --> 01:32:07.290
You're gonna gather a lot of data

01:32:07.290 --> 01:32:09.100
and then you're gonna reconsider.

01:32:09.100 --> 01:32:10.717
And you can think of that

01:32:10.717 --> 01:32:11.870
as a kind of asynchronous updating

01:32:11.870 --> 01:32:14.310
because you aren't updating
every time you gather evidence.

01:32:14.310 --> 01:32:16.880
You're updating only
after a certain amount

01:32:16.880 --> 01:32:19.510
of evidence has been
gathered by each agent.

01:32:19.510 --> 01:32:24.510
And that absolutely
changes the various things

01:32:24.895 --> 01:32:26.150
in the models.

01:32:26.150 --> 01:32:27.910
It doesn't change the qualitative effects

01:32:27.910 --> 01:32:29.090
that these are the kinds of outcomes.

01:32:29.090 --> 01:32:31.000
And sometimes you get this
and sometimes you get that,

01:32:31.000 --> 01:32:32.210
but it does change things

01:32:32.210 --> 01:32:34.059
like the rate of which agents learn,

01:32:34.059 --> 01:32:37.253
which outcomes are more
likely, things like that.

01:32:38.442 --> 01:32:43.442
Other kinds of asynchronous
learning might involve things

01:32:43.520 --> 01:32:45.360
like only sharing every so often

01:32:45.360 --> 01:32:47.530
even though you're updating more often

01:32:47.530 --> 01:32:50.840
or some agents share
and other agents don't.

01:32:50.840 --> 01:32:52.590
One thing that we look at as a situation

01:32:52.590 --> 01:32:56.739
where only some agents are
able to gather information.

01:32:56.739 --> 01:32:58.870
And so some agents are updating only

01:32:58.870 --> 01:33:00.593
on evidence gathered by others.

01:33:01.710 --> 01:33:04.290
We treat that as a situation

01:33:04.290 --> 01:33:06.521
where say you have some policymakers

01:33:06.521 --> 01:33:07.354
and you have some scientists

01:33:07.354 --> 01:33:09.557
and the policy makers are updating

01:33:09.557 --> 01:33:10.390
on the scientist's evidence.

01:33:11.560 --> 01:33:16.560
And all of those sorts of
things can have an effect.

01:33:17.600 --> 01:33:20.730
But they, I think at
the level of description

01:33:20.730 --> 01:33:22.150
that I've given here,

01:33:22.150 --> 01:33:24.863
they don't matter to any of
the results that I've reported.

01:33:27.680 --> 01:33:32.680
- Great, so there's one other
question from Tyler Lutes

01:33:32.720 --> 01:33:35.070
who is a former Franke fellow.

01:33:35.070 --> 01:33:38.220
Polarization might also
be socially advantageous

01:33:38.220 --> 01:33:42.120
in situations in which
truth itself, for instance,

01:33:42.120 --> 01:33:46.300
the relative success of A
versus B changes with time.

01:33:46.300 --> 01:33:47.220
Do you have a sense

01:33:47.220 --> 01:33:50.920
of how your models behave in
such dynamic circumstances?

01:33:50.920 --> 01:33:53.810
I have in mind here climate
change in particular

01:33:53.810 --> 01:33:57.710
in which most of us believe
that true distribution

01:33:57.710 --> 01:34:01.303
of climactic phenomenon is
changing right before our eyes.

01:34:06.145 --> 01:34:10.986
- So I think that the
answer, so we haven't looked

01:34:10.986 --> 01:34:15.986
at situations where the
actual success rates change.

01:34:17.520 --> 01:34:20.363
I mean, it's an interesting
modification to the model,

01:34:21.816 --> 01:34:23.593
but one that we haven't looked at.

01:34:25.300 --> 01:34:28.497
Kevin Goldman though, in
one of his earliest papers

01:34:28.497 --> 01:34:33.497
on network epistemology, really
emphasized the importance

01:34:33.930 --> 01:34:36.880
of what he called transient
diversity of beliefs.

01:34:36.880 --> 01:34:37.930
But you might think also

01:34:37.930 --> 01:34:41.760
as transient polarization,
you know, and I mentioned this

01:34:41.760 --> 01:34:44.090
in response to a previous
comment early on.

01:34:44.090 --> 01:34:46.540
Oh no, to an earlier question,

01:34:46.540 --> 01:34:48.770
the idea that under some circumstances,

01:34:48.770 --> 01:34:50.770
polarization may actually be beneficial.

01:34:52.403 --> 01:34:55.067
And so Kevin's suggestion
there is that basically,

01:34:58.950 --> 01:35:01.504
if instead of thinking of the,

01:35:01.504 --> 01:35:06.220
a situation where the
actual outcomes change,

01:35:06.220 --> 01:35:08.620
if you think about a
situation where over time,

01:35:08.620 --> 01:35:12.433
you you get access to new or
different kinds of evidence.

01:35:13.860 --> 01:35:16.320
Having people who
continue to hold the thing

01:35:16.320 --> 01:35:20.040
that seems incompatible
with the old evidence means

01:35:20.040 --> 01:35:23.730
that they'll sort of pursue
new lines of evidence.

01:35:23.730 --> 01:35:26.120
And the example that he
gives is the discovery

01:35:26.120 --> 01:35:29.147
of the bacterial origins of ulcers.

01:35:32.180 --> 01:35:33.150
And so it turns out

01:35:33.150 --> 01:35:35.180
that there's a bacterium called H. Pylori

01:35:35.180 --> 01:35:36.873
that causes ulcers.

01:35:38.819 --> 01:35:42.876
By the 1960s, the idea of a
bacterial cause of ulcers was

01:35:42.876 --> 01:35:47.876
basically ruled out by a series of,

01:35:49.564 --> 01:35:53.350
really a small number of
very influential experiments

01:35:53.350 --> 01:35:54.600
that claim to have shown

01:35:54.600 --> 01:35:58.400
that the stomach is antibacterial.

01:36:00.750 --> 01:36:03.020
Basically, that there
aren't bacterial cultures

01:36:03.020 --> 01:36:05.010
in the stomach, and so you can't have

01:36:05.010 --> 01:36:06.843
a bacterial cause of ulcers.

01:36:08.340 --> 01:36:11.837
And, you know, one of the, you know,

01:36:11.837 --> 01:36:16.120
people stopped pursuing
that line of research except

01:36:16.120 --> 01:36:19.610
in Australia, where they
were just disconnected enough

01:36:19.610 --> 01:36:21.050
from the rest of the research community.

01:36:21.050 --> 01:36:21.900
They had their own

01:36:21.900 --> 01:36:24.110
sort of autonomous research program going,

01:36:24.110 --> 01:36:26.410
and they continued to
explore this possibility

01:36:26.410 --> 01:36:28.310
and eventually identified
a kind of bacteria

01:36:28.310 --> 01:36:30.453
that hadn't been known before.

01:36:31.743 --> 01:36:35.450
And, you know, we were able
to establish that in fact,

01:36:35.450 --> 01:36:37.037
that is present in stomachs.

01:36:37.037 --> 01:36:41.939
And in fact it does
cause ulcers, you know,

01:36:41.939 --> 01:36:43.655
and the guy who would ultimately--

01:36:43.655 --> 01:36:44.488
- I was gonna say, this is a famous case

01:36:44.488 --> 01:36:47.250
where the experimenter actually took--

01:36:47.250 --> 01:36:50.100
- Drank H. Pylori.

01:36:50.100 --> 01:36:52.880
- Yeah, drank the H. Pylori.

01:36:52.880 --> 01:36:54.020
- Then took an antibiotic.

01:36:54.020 --> 01:36:56.270
So he gave himself an
ulcer by drinking H. Pylori

01:36:56.270 --> 01:36:57.970
and the cured it with antibiotics.

01:36:57.970 --> 01:37:00.310
And it's not clear what
the epistemic value

01:37:00.310 --> 01:37:02.097
of that is, but it's (mumbles).

01:37:05.481 --> 01:37:07.490
- I think desperation science, right.

01:37:07.490 --> 01:37:08.333
To persuade.

01:37:10.050 --> 01:37:14.293
So I guess if there are
no further questions,

01:37:15.270 --> 01:37:18.370
I would really like to thank James

01:37:18.370 --> 01:37:20.810
for an excellent thought-provoking talk.

01:37:20.810 --> 01:37:24.424
And we really look forward
to the discussion tomorrow

01:37:24.424 --> 01:37:28.040
with Professor Weatherall and Kevin Dorst.

01:37:28.040 --> 01:37:32.940
So at this point, I just want
to invite everyone to unmute

01:37:32.940 --> 01:37:36.553
and clap if possible.

01:37:38.699 --> 01:37:39.667
- Thank you Priya.

01:37:39.667 --> 01:37:41.140
- Thank you so much.

01:37:41.140 --> 01:37:43.280
- [James] Look forward
to seeing you tomorrow.

01:37:43.280 --> 01:37:46.683
- See you all tomorrow
